OAI_PAPER({"slug": "Finite-generation-for-the-Kn-local-sphere-September-24-2026", "title": "Finite generation for the K(n)-local sphere", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/Finite-generation-for-the-Kn-local-sphere-September-24-2026/Finite-generation-for-the-Kn-local-sphere-September-24-2026.pdf", "family": "313", "family_title": "Finite generation for the \\(K(n)\\)-local sphere", "abstract_html": "We prove that the homotopy groups of the \\(K(n)\\)-local sphere are finitely generated ℤ<sub><i>p</i></sub>-modules in every integer degree, for every prime <i>p</i> and positive height <i>n</i>. Equivalently, the same conclusion holds after \\(K(n)\\)-localizing any finite <i>p</i>-local spectrum. This answers the degreewise finiteness question of Hovey and Hovey–Strickland.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "Theorem \\(1.1\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The \\(\\mathbb Z_p\\)-module \\( \\pi_tL_{K(n)}S_p^\\wedge \\) is finitely generated.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(p\\) be a prime, \\(n\\geq 1\\), and \\(t\\in\\mathbb Z\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(K(n)\\) denote Morava \\(K\\)-theory of height \\(n\\) at \\(p\\), let \\(L_{K(n)}\\) denote localization, and let \\(S_p^\\wedge\\) be the \\(p\\)-completed sphere.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The \\(\\mathbb Z_p\\)-module\n\\[\n\\pi_tL_{K(n)}S_p^\\wedge\n\\]\nis finitely generated. Equivalently, for every finite \\(p\\)-local spectrum \\(F\\), the module\n\\[\n\\pi_tL_{K(n)}F\n\\]\nis finitely generated over \\(\\mathbb Z_p\\) in every integer degree.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(X=L_{K(n)}S_p^\\wedge\\). Every \\(K(n)\\)-local spectrum is derived \\(p\\)-complete for \\(n&gt;0\\): the fiber of\n\\[\nX\\to\\operatorname*{holim}_aX/p^a\n\\]\nis\n\\[\n\\operatorname*{holim}(\\cdots\\xrightarrow pX\\xrightarrow pX)\n\\simeq F(S[1/p],X),\n\\]\nwhich vanishes because \\(S[1/p]\\) is \\(K(n)\\)-acyclic.</p><p>The map \\(S\\to S_p^\\wedge\\) is a mod-\\(p\\) equivalence and hence a \\(K(n)\\)-equivalence: its cofiber has invertible \\(p\\), whereas \\(p\\) acts as zero on Morava \\(K\\)-theory. Exactness of localization gives\n\\[\nX\\simeq L_{K(n)}S,\n\\qquad\nX/p\\simeq L_{K(n)}(S/p).\n\\]\nProposition \\(29\\) makes all homotopy groups of \\(X/p\\) finite; Proposition \\(30\\) proves the sphere assertion.</p><p>For the finite-spectrum formulation, the finite \\(p\\)-local spectra \\(F\\) for which\n\\[\n\\pi_jL_{K(n)}(F\\wedge S/p)\n\\]\nis finite in every degree form a class closed under suspensions, finite cofiber sequences, and retracts. Proposition \\(29\\) puts the sphere in this class, so it contains every finite \\(p\\)-local spectrum. The same locality argument makes \\(L_{K(n)}F\\) derived \\(p\\)-complete. Proposition \\(30\\) applies objectwise.</p><p>The equivalence of the two formulations also follows from finite constructions and preservation of finite generation under kernels, cokernels, and extensions over the Noetherian ring \\(\\mathbb Z_p\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 2", "orig": "Corollary \\(12.3\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( r_{p,n,j} = \\#\\left\\{ I\\subseteq\\{1,\\ldots,n\\}: \\sum_{i\\in I}(1-2i)=j \\right\\}. \\) Equivalently, the free ranks are the graded dimensions of the exterior \\(\\mathbb Q_p\\)-algebra on generators in degrees …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(p\\) be a prime, \\(n\\geq 1\\), and \\(j\\in\\mathbb Z\\).</p><p class=\"li\">By Theorem \\(1\\), write\n\\[\n\\pi_jL_{K(n)}S_p^\\wedge\n\\cong\n\\mathbb Z_p^{\\,r_{p,n,j}}\n\\oplus\n\\bigoplus_{e\\geq 1}(\\mathbb Z/p^e)^{m_{p,n,j,e}},\n\\]\nwith only finitely many nonzero torsion multiplicities.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nr_{p,n,j}\n=\n\\#\\left\\{\nI\\subseteq\\{1,\\ldots,n\\}:\n\\sum_{i\\in I}(1-2i)=j\n\\right\\}.\n\\]\nEquivalently, the free ranks are the graded dimensions of the exterior \\(\\mathbb Q_p\\)-algebra on generators in degrees\n\\[\n-1,-3,\\ldots,-(2n-1).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Barthel–Schlank–Stapleton–Weinstein’s rationalization theorem identifies\n\\[\n\\mathbb Q\\otimes_{\\mathbb Z}\\pi_*L_{K(n)}S\n\\cong\n\\Lambda_{\\mathbb Q_p}(\\zeta_1,\\ldots,\\zeta_n),\n\\qquad |\\zeta_i|=1-2i.\n\\]\nBy Theorem \\(1\\), each integral homotopy group is finitely generated over \\(\\mathbb Z_p\\); its dimension after inverting \\(p\\) is its free rank. Exterior monomials are indexed by subsets \\(I\\subseteq\\{1,\\ldots,n\\}\\), with degree \\(\\sum_{i\\in I}(1-2i)\\). Counting these monomials gives the formula.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 3", "orig": "Proposition \\(12.4\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every \\(j\\in\\mathbb Z\\), \\( \\pi_jL_{K(1)}S_p^\\wedge \\cong \\begin{cases} \\mathbb Z_p,&j=0\\text{ or }j=-1,\\\\ \\mathbb Z/p^{\\,1+\\nu_p(t)},& j=2t-1,\\quad 0\\neq t\\in(p-1)\\mathbb Z,\\\\ 0,&\\text{otherwise}. \\end{cases} \\) …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(p\\) be an odd prime.</p><p class=\"li\">For a nonzero integer \\(t\\), let \\(\\nu_p(t)\\) be its \\(p\\)-adic valuation.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(j\\in\\mathbb Z\\),\n\\[\n\\pi_jL_{K(1)}S_p^\\wedge\n\\cong\n\\begin{cases}\n\\mathbb Z_p,&amp;j=0\\text{ or }j=-1,\\\\\n\\mathbb Z/p^{\\,1+\\nu_p(t)},&amp;\nj=2t-1,\\quad 0\\neq t\\in(p-1)\\mathbb Z,\\\\\n0,&amp;\\text{otherwise}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nThese groups include all additive extensions.</p>", "proof": "<p>At height one,\n\\[\n\\pi_*KU_p^\\wedge=\\mathbb Z_p[\\beta^{\\pm1}],\n\\qquad |\\beta|=2,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\mathbb Z_p^\\times=\\mu_{p-1}\\times(1+p\\mathbb Z_p).\n\\]\nThe Adams operation \\(\\psi^a\\) acts on \\(\\beta^t\\) by \\(a^t\\).</p><p>Taking \\(\\mu_{p-1}\\)-homotopy fixed points is exact on these coefficients because \\(p-1\\) is a unit. The resulting Adams summand \\(B\\) has\n\\[\n\\pi_{2t}B=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\mathbb Z_p,&amp;(p-1)\\mid t,\\\\\n0,&amp;(p-1)\\nmid t,\n\\end{cases}\n\\qquad\n\\pi_{2t+1}B=0.\n\\]\nThe element \\(1+p\\) topologically generates \\(1+p\\mathbb Z_p\\). Its two-term continuous resolution, equivalently the standard Adams-operation fiber sequence, gives\n\\[\nL_{K(1)}S_p^\\wedge\\longrightarrow B\n\\xrightarrow{\\psi^{1+p}-1}B.\n\\]\nOn a retained degree \\(2t\\), the map is multiplication by \\((1+p)^t-1\\).</p><p>For \\(t=0\\), it is zero, yielding \\(\\mathbb Z_p\\) in degrees \\(0,-1\\). For \\(t\\neq0\\), it is injective and\n\\[\n\\nu_p((1+p)^t-1)=1+\\nu_p(t).\n\\]\nFor \\(p\\nmid a\\), binomial expansion gives\n\\[\n(1+p)^a-1\\equiv ap\\pmod{p^2}.\n\\]\nIf \\(\\nu_p(z)\\geq1\\), the term \\(pz\\) in \\((1+z)^p-1\\) has valuation \\(1+\\nu_p(z)\\), and all other terms have larger valuation because \\(p\\) is odd. Iteration proves the formula for positive \\(t\\). For negative \\(t\\), use\n\\[\n(1+p)^{-t}-1=-\\frac{(1+p)^t-1}{(1+p)^t}.\n\\]</p><p>Thus each nonzero retained \\(t\\) contributes the stated cyclic cokernel in degree \\(2t-1\\), and no kernel in degree \\(2t\\). Other coefficients vanish. There is at most one kernel or cokernel in each stem, so no additive extension remains unresolved.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "Lemma \\(2.3\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Every compact subset of a step lies in a compact \\(G\\)-stable \\(k\\)-subspace \\(N\\) contained in one complete step.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(k\\) be a finite field of characteristic \\(p\\), and let \\(G\\) be a compact \\(p\\)-adic analytic group.</p><p class=\"li\">A coefficient module with complete steps, as in Definition \\(2.1\\), is a vector space\n\\[\nM=\\bigcup_{a\\geq 0}M_a\n\\]\nwhose steps are complete Hausdorff linearly topologized \\(k\\)-spaces, with continuous injective transition maps. For every \\(a\\), some \\(b\\) satisfies \\(GM_a\\subseteq M_b\\), and \\(G\\times M_a\\to M_b\\) is continuous.</p><p class=\"li\">Morphisms obey the analogous bounded-step continuity condition.</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n\\[\nC_{\\mathrm{cts}}^j(G,M)\n=\n\\operatorname*{colim}_a C_{\\mathrm{cts}}(G^j,M_a),\n\\qquad\nH^j(G,M)=H^jC_{\\mathrm{cts}}^\\bullet(G,M).\n\\]\nCompact lattices use their adic topology; localizations use bounded-denominator steps.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p class=\"li num\">1. Every compact subset of a step lies in a compact \\(G\\)-stable \\(k\\)-subspace \\(N\\) contained in one complete step. These subspaces form a filtered system covering \\(M\\), and\n\\[\nC_{\\mathrm{cts}}^\\bullet(G,M)\n=\n\\operatorname*{colim}_N C_{\\mathrm{cts}}^\\bullet(G,N).\n\\]</p><p class=\"li num\">2. The ring \\(k[[G]]\\) is Noetherian. The trivial compact module \\(k\\) has a possibly unbounded resolution \\(P_\\bullet\\) by finitely generated free compact \\(k[[G]]\\)-modules, and\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Hom}_{k[[G]],\\mathrm{cts}}(P_\\bullet,M)\n\\simeq C_{\\mathrm{cts}}^\\bullet(G,M).\n\\]\nEvery term on the left is a finite power of \\(M\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">3. A right resolution similarly defines \\(H_i(G,M)\\). For compact \\(M\\), homology and cohomology are compact Hausdorff; continuous duality exchanges them, using the contragredient action on\n\\[\nM^\\vee=\\operatorname{Hom}_{k,\\mathrm{cts}}(M,k).\n\\]</p><p class=\"li num\">4. For \\(G=\\mathbb Z_p\\), with generator \\(\\gamma\\), the cochain complex is\n\\[\n[M\\xrightarrow{\\gamma-1}M]\n\\]\nin degrees \\(0,1\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For a compact \\(T\\subseteq M_a\\), the set \\(GT\\) is compact in some \\(M_b\\). Its closed linear span is totally bounded: modulo any open linear subspace, the image of \\(GT\\), and hence its span over finite \\(k\\), is finite. Completeness makes the span compact. Continuity of the action into a common enlarged step shows that this span is \\(G\\)-stable and that the restricted action is continuous. Finite sums give filteredness. Compact cochain images lie in these envelopes.</p><p>Choose a normal uniform open subgroup \\(K\\subseteq G\\). The augmentation-filtered associated graded ring of \\(k[[K]]\\) is a polynomial ring on \\(\\dim G\\) variables, so \\(k[[K]]\\), and then the finite crossed-product extension \\(k[[G]]\\), are Noetherian. Resolve successive finitely generated kernels to obtain \\(P_\\bullet\\).</p><p>For a compact envelope \\(N\\), compare \\(P_\\bullet\\) with the compact bar resolution. Compact \\(k\\)-spaces are projective by continuous duality; completed induction preserves projectivity. The augmented resolutions split as compact \\(k\\)-complexes, so comparison maps and homotopies are continuous. Applying continuous Hom computes bar cochains.</p><p>The \\(k[[G]]\\)-action on \\(M\\) is defined by integration inside compact \\(G\\)-stable envelopes. Since each \\(P_j\\) is finite free, its Hom into \\(M\\) is the filtered union of its Hom into the envelopes. Exactness of filtered colimits gives the comparison for \\(M\\). The same argument applies to right resolutions and homology.</p><p>For compact coefficients, differentials have compact, hence closed, images in finite powers. Continuous duality is exact and exchanges the complexes. Finally,\n\\[\nk[[\\mathbb Z_p]]=k[[\\gamma-1]]\n\\]\nhas the asserted two-term resolution.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "Lemma \\(2.2\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The sequence of bounded-step continuous cochain complexes is short exact and gives the usual long exact cohomology sequence.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the complete-step convention of Proposition \\(1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n\\[\n0\\longrightarrow M'\\xrightarrow{\\iota}M\\xrightarrow{q}M''\\longrightarrow 0\n\\]\nbe exact on underlying vector spaces, with maps and actions respecting complete steps.</p><p class=\"li\">Assume:</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">1. Every bounded-step continuous map from a compact profinite space into \\(M\\), with image in \\(\\iota(M')\\), is bounded-step continuous into \\(M'\\).</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">2. For every compact subset \\(T\\) of a step of \\(M''\\), there is a continuous section \\(s:T\\to M_b\\) of \\(q\\), for some \\(b\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The sequence of bounded-step continuous cochain complexes is short exact and gives the usual long exact cohomology sequence.</p><p>These hypotheses hold for strict short exact sequences of compact \\(k\\)-spaces, finite modules over a complete Noetherian local \\(k\\)-algebra with finite residue field, and their bounded-denominator localizations.</p>", "proof": "<p>The kernel assertion is the first hypothesis. A quotient cochain has compact image \\(T\\) in one step; composing it with \\(s\\) lifts that cochain. No equivariance of \\(s\\) is required.</p><p>For compact vector spaces, continuous duality with discrete vector spaces turns a strict surjection into an injection. Split that injection as a vector-space map and dualize to obtain a continuous linear section.</p><p>For finite modules over a complete Noetherian local ring, submodules are closed and Artin–Rees identifies their induced and adic topologies. For a localized quotient lattice, choose lifts of finitely many generators and a common denominator. The resulting compact source lattice surjects strictly and admits a continuous linear section. Artin–Rees also verifies the kernel condition after enlarging the denominator.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "Proposition \\(2.6\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every trace-admissible \\(M\\), \\( H^j(G,M)=0\\qquad(j>a_Gd_G). \\) The same bound holds for \\(M\\otimes_k V\\) for every finite-dimensional continuous \\(G\\)-representation \\(V\\), with \\(K\\) and the bound independent of …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(G\\) be compact \\(p\\)-adic analytic, \\(k\\) finite of characteristic \\(p\\), and \\(K\\triangleleft G\\) a normal uniform open subgroup.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n\\[\nd_G=\\dim G,\\qquad a_G=[G:K].\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">As in Definition \\(2.4\\), a complete-step semilinear module \\(M\\) over a commutative \\(G\\)-algebra \\(A\\) is trace-admissible if every finite subgroup \\(F\\subseteq G\\) has \\(t_F\\in A\\) with\n\\[\n\\sum_{f\\in F}f(t_F)=1,\n\\]\nand multiplication by \\(t_F\\) is continuous between suitable complete steps.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every trace-admissible \\(M\\),\n\\[\nH^j(G,M)=0\\qquad(j&gt;a_Gd_G).\n\\]\nThe same bound holds for \\(M\\otimes_k V\\) for every finite-dimensional continuous \\(G\\)-representation \\(V\\), with \\(K\\) and the bound independent of \\(M,V\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For a finite subgroup \\(F\\) and a semilinear module \\(L\\), a trace-one element \\(t\\) gives an \\(F\\)-linear map\n\\[\nL\\longrightarrow k[F]\\otimes_kL,\n\\qquad\nv\\longmapsto\\sum_{f\\in F}f\\otimes t f^{-1}v.\n\\]\nThe map \\(f\\otimes v\\mapsto fv\\) retracts it, since the composite multiplies by \\(\\sum_f f(t)=1\\). Thus \\(L\\) is a summand of an induced module, and \\(H^{&gt;0}(F,L)=0\\). The formulas are finite sums of bounded continuous operators and also apply to function modules and finite twists.</p><p>The trivial \\(k[[K]]\\)-module has a finite free resolution of length \\(d_G\\), by uniform-group duality. Tensor-induce it over the \\(a_G\\) cosets to obtain a compact \\(k\\)-split resolution \\(T_\\bullet\\to k\\), concentrated in degrees \\(0,\\ldots,a_Gd_G\\). Its terms are finite sums of signed permutation modules \\(k[[X]]\\), where \\(X\\) is a finite union of products of copies of \\(K\\). The diagonal \\(K\\)-action is free and has a continuous trivialization.</p><p>The module\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Hom}_{k,\\mathrm{cts}}(k[[X]],M)=C_{\\mathrm{cts}}(X,M)\n\\]\nis continuously coinduced over \\(K\\), after untwisting values, so its positive \\(K\\)-cohomology vanishes. Hochschild–Serre reduces its \\(G\\)-cohomology to finite-group cohomology on \\(K\\)-invariants.</p><p>Lemma \\(2.1\\) gives local stabilizer-equivariant sections over clopen neighborhoods in \\(K\\backslash X\\). Separate the finitely many translates outside each stabilizer, and refine a finite cover to a disjoint invariant clopen partition. Each piece is induced from a finite stabilizer, whose positive cohomology vanishes by trace splitting. Hence\n\\[\nH^j\\!\\left(G,\\operatorname{Hom}_{k,\\mathrm{cts}}(k[[X]],M)\\right)=0\n\\quad(j&gt;0).\n\\]</p><p>The double complex\n\\[\nC_{\\mathrm{cts}}^u\\!\\left(G,\\operatorname{Hom}_{k,\\mathrm{cts}}(T_v,M)\\right)\n\\]\ncomputes \\(H^*(G,M)\\) in one direction and, in the other, is supported in \\(u=0\\), \\(0\\leq v\\leq a_Gd_G\\). Compact-envelope integration from Proposition \\(1\\) makes the contracting homotopies legitimate on complete steps. Finite twists preserve all hypotheses.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "Lemma \\(2.5\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The finite stabilizer \\(G_x\\) maps isomorphically to \\(H_y\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(K\\triangleleft G\\) be normal uniform open.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(X\\) be a profinite \\(G\\)-space on which \\(K\\) acts freely.</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose \\(X\\to Y=K\\backslash X\\) has a continuous \\(K\\)-equivariant trivialization.</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(H=G/K\\). Let \\(x\\in X\\) map to \\(y\\in Y\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The finite stabilizer \\(G_x\\) maps isomorphically to \\(H_y\\). There is an \\(H_y\\)-invariant clopen neighborhood \\(V\\) of \\(y\\) and a section \\(s:V\\to X\\), with \\(s(y)=x\\), equivariant for \\(G_x\\cong H_y\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>A lift of an element of \\(H_y\\) carries \\(x\\) into its \\(K\\)-orbit. Correcting by the unique element of \\(K\\) gives an element fixing \\(x\\), proving the stabilizer isomorphism.</p><p>Write \\(F=G_x\\). A continuous section through \\(x\\) has an equivariance defect given by a continuous nonabelian cocycle \\(c_f\\in C(V,K)\\), with \\(c_f(y)=1\\). Shrink \\(V\\) so these cocycles lie in an arbitrarily small \\(F\\)-stable Lie lattice.</p><p>Put \\(\\lambda_f=\\log c_f\\) and \\(\\epsilon=\\max_f\\|\\lambda_f\\|\\). Campbell–Hausdorff estimates give\n\\[\n\\|\\lambda_{fg}-\\lambda_f-f\\lambda_g\\|\\leq c\\epsilon^2.\n\\]\nFor\n\\[\nb=|F|^{-1}\\sum_f\\lambda_f,\n\\]\none has\n\\[\n\\|\\lambda_f+fb-b\\|\\leq c\\,||F||_p^{-1}\\epsilon^2,\n\\qquad\n\\|b\\|\\leq ||F||_p^{-1}\\epsilon.\n\\]\nChanging the section by \\(\\exp(-b)\\) makes the new defect quadratically smaller. Choose the initial neighborhood so all changes converge and remain in the Lie lattice. Iteration converges uniformly to an equivariant section still taking \\(y\\) to \\(x\\). Division by \\(|F|\\) occurs only in \\(\\mathbb Q_p\\), with its fixed norm loss absorbed by the initial shrink.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "Proposition \\(2.7\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There are natural isomorphisms \\( H_i(G,M)\\cong H^{d_G-i}(G,M) \\qquad(i\\in\\mathbb Z). \\) In particular \\(H^j(G,M)=0\\) for \\(j>d_G\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(G\\) be compact \\(p\\)-adic analytic, \\(k\\) finite of characteristic \\(p\\), and \\(M\\) trace-admissible in the sense of Proposition \\(2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Assume the orientation character of \\(G\\) is trivial over \\(k\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Homology and cohomology use Proposition \\(1\\), and vanish in negative degrees by convention.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are natural isomorphisms\n\\[\nH_i(G,M)\\cong H^{d_G-i}(G,M)\n\\qquad(i\\in\\mathbb Z).\n\\]\nIn particular \\(H^j(G,M)=0\\) for \\(j&gt;d_G\\).</p><p>For the ordinary Morava stabilizer \\(P_n\\), the orientation is trivial and \\(d_G=n^2\\), so no orientation twist is needed.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose \\(K\\triangleleft G\\) normal uniform open, and write\n\\[\nR=k[[G]],\\quad R_K=k[[K]],\\quad H=G/K,\\quad \\Sigma=k[H].\n\\]\nThe quotient bimodule \\(\\Sigma\\) is perfect over \\(R\\). Uniform-group duality, retaining conjugation and orientation, gives\n\\[\nR\\operatorname{Hom}_R(\\Sigma,R)\\simeq\\Sigma[-d_G]\n\\]\nas a \\((\\Sigma,R)\\)-bimodule: the dual resolution has one orientation line per coset; right translation permutes these regularly, and precomposition gives the left regular action with the orientation character, trivial by assumption.</p><p>Set\n\\[\nC=R\\operatorname{Hom}_R(\\Sigma,M)=R\\Gamma(K,M),\n\\qquad\nB=\\Sigma\\otimes_R^LM.\n\\]\nPerfect evaluation gives \\(C\\simeq B[-d_G]\\). Proposition \\(1\\) identifies these derived expressions with the prescribed complete-step complexes.</p><p>For every finite-dimensional \\(\\Sigma\\)-module \\(V\\),\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Ext}_\\Sigma^j(V,C)\n=\nH^j(G,V^*\\otimes_kM)=0\n\\qquad(j&gt;a_Gd_G)\n\\]\nby Proposition \\(2\\). In a bounded-below injective resolution of \\(C\\), dimension shifting beyond both \\(d_G\\) and \\(a_Gd_G\\), followed by Baer’s criterion, makes the tail kernel injective. Thus \\(C\\) has a bounded injective model.</p><p>Every injective module over a finite group algebra is projective, including in infinite dimension: its canonical injection into \\(\\operatorname{Hom}_k(\\Sigma,L)\\) splits, and the symmetric group-algebra pairing identifies this target with a free induced module. The finite-group norm is an isomorphism from coinvariants to invariants on free modules and hence on projective summands. Applied to the bounded model, it gives\n\\[\nk\\otimes_\\Sigma^LC\\simeq R\\operatorname{Hom}_\\Sigma(k,C).\n\\]\nAfter shifting,\n\\[\n(k\\otimes_\\Sigma^LC)[d_G]\\simeq k\\otimes_R^LM,\n\\qquad\nR\\operatorname{Hom}_\\Sigma(k,C)=R\\Gamma(G,M).\n\\]\nTaking degree \\(-i\\) gives the result.</p><p>For \\(G=P_n\\), its Lie algebra is the division algebra \\(D_n\\). Conjugation by \\(a\\in D_n^\\times\\) has determinant\n\\[\n\\det(L_a)\\det(R_a)^{-1}\n=\n\\operatorname{Nrd}(a)^n\\operatorname{Nrd}(a)^{-n}=1.\n\\]\nIts orientation is therefore trivial and its dimension is \\(n^2\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "Proposition \\(3.4\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There are a finite free \\(A_m\\)-algebra \\(A'\\), with integral multiplication in a fixed basis, and a \\(G\\)-invariant idempotent \\(e\\in B'=A'[1/x]\\), such that \\( B_U=eB',\\qquad B'/B_m\\text{ is finite étale}. \\) The …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(p,n\\), and a finite field \\(k\\) containing the chosen Honda fields of definition.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n\\[\nG=P_n,\\qquad A_m=k[[u_m,\\ldots,u_{n-1}]],\\qquad A_n=k.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal F/A_m\\) be the restricted universal deformation. For \\(m&lt;n\\), put\n\\[\nx=u_m,\\quad B_m=A_m[1/x],\\quad r=n-m.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">If \\(\\alpha_g:g^*\\mathcal F\\to\\mathcal F\\) has derivative \\(\\chi_g\\), let \\(L_m\\) be the semilinear line with this cocycle. Then\n\\[\ng(x)=\\chi_g^{p^m-1}x.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(\\ell\\geq1\\), \\(0\\leq b\\leq m\\ell\\), define\n\\[\nD_m(\\ell,b)\n=\nA_m[T^{p^b}]\n\\subseteq A_m[[T]]/([p^\\ell]_{\\mathcal F}(T)).\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Definition \\(3.1\\): basic coefficients are\n\\[\nL_m^{\\otimes w}\\otimes_{A_m}\n\\bigotimes_{j=1}^sD_m(\\ell_j,b_j),\n\\qquad w\\in\\mathbb Z,\\quad 0\\leq b_j\\leq m\\ell_j,\n\\]\nincluding \\(s=0\\). The allowed class \\(\\mathcal C_m\\) is closed under finite sums, tensor products, equivariant summands, and finite equivariant filtrations.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(J=P_m\\times\\operatorname{GL}_r(\\mathbb Z_p)\\), and let \\(U\\triangleleft J\\) be a sufficiently deep normal product congruence subgroup. Let \\(B_U\\) be its finite algebra of connected and étale markings.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are a finite free \\(A_m\\)-algebra \\(A'\\), with integral multiplication in a fixed basis, and a \\(G\\)-invariant idempotent \\(e\\in B'=A'[1/x]\\), such that\n\\[\nB_U=eB',\\qquad B'/B_m\\text{ is finite étale}.\n\\]\nThe semilinear module \\(A'\\) has a finite filtration with basic quotients in \\(\\mathcal C_m\\), specializing to such a filtration in \\(\\mathcal C_{m+1}\\). The \\(G\\)-action has continuous matrices over \\(A_m\\); in particular the whole-basis action has a uniform pole bound.</p>", "proof": "<p>Relative Frobenius of exponent \\(m\\ell\\) kills the connected part of \\(\\mathcal F[p^\\ell]\\) on the exact-height open. Its image has algebra\n\\[\nD_m(\\ell,m\\ell)[1/x]\n\\]\nand is étale: the linear coefficient of the division series in \\(T^{p^{m\\ell}}\\) is invertible.</p><p>Ordered bases in the \\(r\\)-fold étale division group form an open-and-closed subscheme, with characteristic idempotent preserved by \\(G\\). Take\n\\[\nA'=H_{m,h}\\otimes_{A_m}D_m(\\ell,m\\ell)^{\\otimes r}\n\\]\nfor levels defining \\(U\\), and extend this idempotent across the connected factor. This produces \\(B_U=eA'[1/x]\\).</p><p>Tensor the filtration of Lemma \\(4.2\\) with the whole tuple algebra. Lemma \\(4.1\\) gives its boundary specialization. Tensoring the free bases gives integral multiplication. Coordinate substitution, reduced in this finite free basis, has continuous integral matrix entries: modulo a fixed parameter power only a finite truncation is needed. Hence all group matrices have zero pole loss on the whole lattice. The idempotent is used only after localization.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.1", "orig": "Lemma \\(3.2\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The algebra \\(D_m(\\ell,b)\\) is finite free of rank \\(p^{n\\ell-b}\\), is intrinsic to relative Frobenius, and commutes with restriction to deeper height loci.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use \\(A_m,\\mathcal F,D_m(\\ell,b),\\mathcal C_m\\) from Proposition \\(4\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The algebra \\(D_m(\\ell,b)\\) is finite free of rank \\(p^{n\\ell-b}\\), is intrinsic to relative Frobenius, and commutes with restriction to deeper height loci. Every member of \\(\\mathcal C_m\\) is finite free, and reduction modulo \\(x=u_m\\) sends \\(\\mathcal C_m\\) into \\(\\mathcal C_{m+1}\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Write \\([p^\\ell]_{\\mathcal F}(T)=f(T^{p^b})\\). Modulo the maximal ideal,\n\\[\nf(Z)=Z^{p^{n\\ell-b}}.\n\\]\nWeierstrass preparation gives \\(f=vW\\), with \\(v\\) a unit and \\(W\\) monic distinguished of degree \\(p^{n\\ell-b}\\). Monic division shows that\n\\[\nA_m[Z]/(W)\\longrightarrow\nA_m[[T]]/([p^\\ell](T)),\n\\qquad Z\\longmapsto T^{p^b},\n\\]\nis injective with the asserted basis and rank.</p><p>A coordinate change sends \\(T^{p^b}\\) to a series in \\(T^{p^b}\\), preserving the subalgebra. Monic division commutes with base change, including \\(A_m\\to A_{m+1}\\), and \\(b\\leq m\\ell\\leq(m+1)\\ell\\).</p><p>Basic coefficients are free. An extension with free quotient splits as an underlying module; a finite summand is projective and hence free over the local ring. These splittings also preserve the stated filtrations after reduction.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.2", "orig": "Proposition \\(3.3\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For every \\(h\\geq0\\), the connected marking through exponent \\(p^h\\) has a finite free model \\(H_{m,h}/A_m\\), with coordinates \\(a_0,\\ldots,a_h\\) satisfying …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Assume \\(m&lt;n\\) and use Proposition \\(4\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Choose Honda coordinates with \\([p]_{\\Gamma_m}(T)=T^{p^m}\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(h\\geq0\\), the connected marking through exponent \\(p^h\\) has a finite free model \\(H_{m,h}/A_m\\), with coordinates \\(a_0,\\ldots,a_h\\) satisfying\n\\[\na_0^{p^m-1}=x,\n\\qquad\na_i^{p^m}-x^{p^i}a_i=P_i(a_0,\\ldots,a_{i-1})\n\\quad(1\\leq i\\leq h).\n\\]\nIts basis is\n\\[\na_0^{e_0}\\cdots a_h^{e_h},\n\\qquad\n0\\leq e_0&lt;p^m-1,\\quad 0\\leq e_i&lt;p^m\\ (i&gt;0).\n\\]\nThe \\(G\\)-action preserves this model and has a filtration with quotients integer powers of \\(L_m\\), specializing to every deeper height locus.</p>", "proof": "<p>Write a marking \\(f:\\mathcal F\\to\\Gamma_m\\) as an ordinary power series, with \\(a_i\\) its coefficient at \\(T^{p^i}\\). At a degree not a power of \\(p\\), a nonzero intermediate binomial coefficient determines the coefficient polynomially from earlier coefficients. Comparing\n\\[\nf([p]_{\\mathcal F}(T))=f(T)^{p^m}\n\\]\ngives the displayed triangular equations.</p><p>Successive monic division gives the basis and rank \\((p^m-1)p^{mh}\\). After inverting \\(x\\), all derivatives of the defining equations are units, so the model is finite étale. The actual finite marking quotient has this same rank by the Honda automorphism filtration. Jet coefficients distinguish geometric jets, so the resulting surjection of finite étale algebras of equal rank is an isomorphism.</p><p>Precomposition gives\n\\[\ng(a_i)=\\chi_g^{p^i}a_i+Q_{g,i}(a_0,\\ldots,a_{i-1}),\n\\qquad\ng(a_0)=\\chi_ga_0.\n\\]\nThe relations are preserved after localization and therefore integrally by \\(x\\)-torsion-freeness. Order the basis lexicographically starting with the highest index. Earlier-coordinate terms and monic reductions lower this order; diagonal characters are\n\\[\n\\chi_g^{e_0+pe_1+\\cdots+p^he_h}.\n\\]\nInitial spans give the desired free filtration. All formulas use finitely many coefficients at fixed jet order and are continuous in \\(g\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "Proposition \\(3.5\\) and Lemma \\(3.6\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "At division level \\(\\ell\\), the reduced closure of the lifted geometric étale bases has algebra \\( R_{m,\\ell}=k[[t_{1,\\ell},\\ldots,t_{r,\\ell}]]. \\) For \\(s_i=t_{i,\\ell}^{p^{m\\ell}}\\) …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(1\\leq m&lt;n\\), \\(r=n-m\\), and the deformation setting of Proposition \\(4\\).</p><p class=\"li\">At full markings, compatible lifts of the ordered étale generators form a torsor under\n\\[\nT=\\varprojlim_{[p]}\\Gamma_m[p^\\ell]^r.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p class=\"li num\">1. At division level \\(\\ell\\), the reduced closure of the lifted geometric étale bases has algebra\n\\[\nR_{m,\\ell}=k[[t_{1,\\ell},\\ldots,t_{r,\\ell}]].\n\\]\nFor \\(s_i=t_{i,\\ell}^{p^{m\\ell}}\\),\n\\[\nS_{m,\\ell}=A_m[s_1,\\ldots,s_r]=k[[s_1,\\ldots,s_r]].\n\\]\nAfter inverting \\(x\\), these are respectively the full generator-lift algebra and the étale basis algebra. The root extension has rank \\(p^{m\\ell r}\\), with no reduction of the lift scheme omitted.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. The constructions are natural under \\(G\\), changes of markings, and finite étale base change. Earlier division coordinates are integral series in the indicated powers of later coordinates. In first-level lift coordinates the base parameters have order at least \\(p^m\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">3. Each basic coefficient of \\(\\mathcal C_m\\), after full markings, is associated to a finite-dimensional translation representation over \\(k\\). Any fixed finite collection of its torsors, representations, and maps descends to one sufficiently deep finite marking level. The periodicity line trivializes at the linear connected jet.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma \\(5.1\\) proves the full root-algebra statement, including scheme structure and naturality. Lemma \\(5.2\\) supplies its application to basic coefficients and the finite-stage descent of their finite diagrams.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "Proposition \\(3.5\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The root-algebra descriptions, ranks, naturality, and coordinate-order assertions in parts \\(1\\) and \\(2\\) of Proposition \\(5\\) hold.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the division and marking setting of Proposition \\(5\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The root-algebra descriptions, ranks, naturality, and coordinate-order assertions in parts \\(1\\) and \\(2\\) of Proposition \\(5\\) hold.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(C_\\ell\\) be the whole \\(r\\)-fold division tuple algebra, \\(Q_\\ell\\) the étale basis algebra over \\(B_m\\), and \\(L_\\ell\\) its full generator-lift algebra. Define\n\\[\nR=\\operatorname{image}\\bigl(C_\\ell\\to(L_\\ell)_{\\mathrm{red}}\\bigr).\n\\]\nIt is finite over \\(A_m\\), reduced, complete local with residue field \\(k\\), and all its components dominate \\(B_m\\). Thus \\(\\dim R=r\\).</p><p>Write \\(t_i=t_{i,\\ell}\\), \\(t_{i,1}=[p^{\\ell-1}]_{\\mathcal F}(t_i)\\). The division divisor gives\n\\[\n[p]_{\\mathcal F}(Z)\n=\nv(Z)\n\\prod_{a\\in\\mathbb F_p^r}\n\\left(\nZ-\\sum_{i=1}^r{}_{\\mathcal F}[a_i](t_{i,1})\n\\right)^{p^m}.\n\\]\nEquality holds in \\(R[[Z]]\\) because \\(R\\) is reduced and each component meets the generic basis locus. Modulo \\((t_1,\\ldots,t_r)\\), successive Hasse-coefficient comparisons force all \\(u_m,\\ldots,u_{n-1}\\) to vanish. Hence the maximal ideal is generated by the \\(r\\) elements \\(t_i\\). Completeness gives a surjection \\(k[[T_1,\\ldots,T_r]]\\to R\\); dimension \\(r\\) forces its kernel to vanish.</p><p>Set \\(h=p^{m\\ell}\\), \\(s_i=t_i^h\\), and \\(S=A_m[s_1,\\ldots,s_r]\\). It is a finite complete local \\(A_m\\)-submodule of \\(R\\), of dimension \\(r\\). The quantities \\(t_{i,1}^{p^m}\\) and their formal-group combinations are integral series in the \\(s_i\\). Write \\([p]_{\\mathcal F}(Z)=V(Z^{p^m})\\) and divide its monic division divisor in \\(S[[Z^{p^m}]]\\). The remainder vanishes after embedding in \\(R\\), hence in \\(S\\). Hasse comparison now puts all base parameters in \\((s_1,\\ldots,s_r)\\), proving\n\\[\nS=k[[s_1,\\ldots,s_r]].\n\\]</p><p>The basis condition gives \\(Q_\\ell\\to S[1/x]\\). Its composite into \\((L_\\ell)_{\\mathrm{red}}\\) is injective by faithful flatness and reducedness of \\(Q_\\ell\\), so it is an isomorphism. The extension \\(S\\to R\\) is free on \\(t_1^{e_1}\\cdots t_r^{e_r}\\), \\(0\\leq e_i&lt;h\\), of rank \\(h^r\\). The full lift torsor has the same rank; its surjection onto \\(R[1/x]\\) is therefore an isomorphism.</p><p>Relative Frobenius and division transitions commute with all group isomorphisms. For an étale algebra \\(E/S[1/x]\\), relative Frobenius gives\n\\[\nE\\otimes_{S[1/x]}R[1/x]\\cong E^{1/h}.\n\\]\nFinally,\n\\[\nt_{i,j}=[p^{\\ell-j}](t_{i,\\ell})\n\\]\nis a series in the \\(p^{m(\\ell-j)}\\)-powers, yielding the transition and base-order assertions.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.2", "orig": "Lemma \\(3.6\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "At full markings, \\(M\\) is associated to a finite-dimensional translation representation; its finite data descend as in part \\(3\\) of Proposition \\(5\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use Proposition \\(5\\) and Lemma \\(5.1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(M\\) be a basic coefficient of \\(\\mathcal C_m\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>At full markings, \\(M\\) is associated to a finite-dimensional translation representation; its finite data descend as in part \\(3\\) of Proposition \\(5\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose \\(L\\) at least as large as all division levels in \\(M\\). Lifts of the marked étale generators split the connected–étale extension at level \\(L\\), giving\n\\[\n\\Gamma_m[p^L]\\times(\\mathbb Z/p^L)^r.\n\\]\nChanging lifts translates this splitting by \\(\\Gamma_m[p^L]^r\\). Its finite-dimensional coordinate algebra, its lower-level restrictions, and its powered-coordinate images therefore give the required translation representations. Torsor descent identifies their associated bundles with the original coefficients.</p><p>The derivative of the connected marking trivializes \\(L_m\\), including all positive and negative powers. All schemes, coactions, and maps use finite division level and finitely many matrix coefficients over finite \\(k\\). Their auxiliary marking actions factor through finite quotients. Finite presentation places any fixed finite collection at one common finite marking stage.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "Proposition \\(3.7\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Every finite subgroup \\(F\\subseteq G\\) acts geometrically freely on \\(\\operatorname{Spec}B_U\\), and there is \\(a_F\\in B_U\\) satisfying \\( \\sum_{f\\in F}f(a_F)=1. \\) Multiplication by \\(a_F\\) is continuous between …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use \\(G=P_n\\), \\(B_U\\), and \\(J=P_m\\times\\operatorname{GL}_r(\\mathbb Z_p)\\) from Proposition \\(4\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Assume \\(U\\) is torsion-free.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every finite subgroup \\(F\\subseteq G\\) acts geometrically freely on \\(\\operatorname{Spec}B_U\\), and there is \\(a_F\\in B_U\\) satisfying\n\\[\n\\sum_{f\\in F}f(a_F)=1.\n\\]\nMultiplication by \\(a_F\\) is continuous between bounded-denominator steps of finite coefficient bundles and on bounded analytic section and compact-support models, including uniform compact profinite parameters. These modules are trace-admissible for Propositions \\(2\\) and \\(3\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Suppose a nonidentity finite-order \\(g\\in G\\) fixes a geometric point. On\n\\[\nR=A_m/(g(a)-a:a\\in A_m),\n\\]\nthe framing becomes an actual automorphism \\(\\gamma\\). The difference endomorphism\n\\[\nf(T)=\\gamma(T)-_{\\mathcal F}T\n\\]\nhas nonzero Honda reduction, of finite order \\(d\\). Weierstrass preparation makes \\(R[[T]]/(f)\\) finite free of rank \\(d\\). Every fixed division scheme\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Spec}R[[T]]/(f,[p^\\ell](T))\n\\]\ntherefore has fiber rank at most \\(d\\).</p><p>Lift the fixed point to full markings. Since the \\(U\\)-coset is fixed, \\(gt=tu\\) for \\(u\\in U\\). If \\(g\\) has order \\(N\\), then \\(u^N=1\\), hence \\(u=1\\). Thus \\(\\gamma\\) fixes the connected height-\\(m\\) formal group and all its division subgroups. Their rank \\(p^{m\\ell}\\) exceeds \\(d\\) for large \\(\\ell\\), a contradiction. Specialization uses the finite prepared algebra; evaluation on connected nilpotent coordinates involves only finite sums.</p><p>For a geometrically free finite-group action on any commutative algebra \\(D\\), put \\(C=D^F\\). The trace image is an ideal \\(I\\subseteq C\\). If \\(I\\subseteq\\mathfrak m\\) for a maximal ideal, integrality of \\(D/C\\) gives a geometric point \\(z:D\\to\\Omega\\) above \\(\\mathfrak m\\). The evaluations\n\\[\ne_f(d\\otimes\\lambda)=z(f(d))\\lambda\n\\]\non \\(D\\otimes_C\\Omega\\) are distinct. Chinese remainders give \\(b\\) with \\(e_1(b)=1\\), \\(e_f(b)=0\\) for \\(f\\neq1\\). Its trace evaluates to \\(1\\), but is zero because all original traces lie in \\(\\mathfrak m\\), a contradiction. Hence \\(I=C\\).</p><p>Apply this to \\(B_U\\). Write \\(a_F=ex^{-c}a\\) in the whole model. Multiplication is a finite matrix over \\(B_m\\), so clearing denominators gives bounded-step continuity. On analytic bounds where \\(x\\) is bounded away from zero it is bounded, preserves supports, and has the same bound in supremum norms with compact parameters.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "Lemma \\(3.8\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For integers \\(a<b\\), \\( H^i(G,x^aL/x^bL) \\) is finite for every \\(i\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(m&lt;n\\), \\(x=u_m\\), and \\(G=P_n\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Assume\n\\[\nH^i(G,N)\\text{ is finite for every }N\\in\\mathcal C_{m+1},\\ i\\geq0.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(L\\in\\mathcal C_m\\), or let \\(L=A'\\otimes_{A_m}M\\), where \\(A'\\) is a whole marking model and \\(M\\in\\mathcal C_m\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For integers \\(a&lt;b\\),\n\\[\nH^i(G,x^aL/x^bL)\n\\]\nis finite for every \\(i\\). For each fixed \\(a\\), localization\n\\[\nH^i(G,x^aL)\\longrightarrow H^i(G,L[1/x])\n\\]\nhas countable kernel and cokernel.</p>", "proof": "<p>Filter the strip by\n\\[\nx^jL/x^{j+1}L\n\\cong\n(L/xL)\\otimes L_{m+1}^{\\otimes j(p^m-1)}.\n\\]\nProposition \\(4\\) puts these layers, or their finite filtrations, in \\(\\mathcal C_{m+1}\\). The assumed finiteness and long exact sequences give finite strip cohomology.</p><p>The quotient\n\\[\nL[1/x]/x^aL\n=\n\\bigcup_{j\\geq0}x^{a-j}L/x^aL\n\\]\nhas, by bounded-denominator cochains, cohomology equal to the filtered colimit of the finite strip cohomologies. It is countable over finite \\(k\\). Lemma \\(1.1\\) makes the localization sequence exact on cochains, so its long exact sequence gives countable kernel and cokernel.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "Theorem \\(4.2\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "After a fixed determinant-height normalization, \\( [Z_\\infty/G]\\cong(N_m^r)_{\\mathrm{ind}}, \\) and \\(Z_\\infty\\to(N_m^r)_{\\mathrm{ind}}\\) is a \\(P_n\\)-torsor.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(1\\leq m&lt;n\\), \\(r=n-m\\), \\(G=P_n\\), and \\(J=P_m\\times\\operatorname{GL}_r(\\mathbb Z_p)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">On the perfected analytic domain\n\\[\nX_m=\\{0&lt;|x|&lt;1,\\ |y_1|,\\ldots,|y_{r-1}|&lt;1\\},\n\\]\nlet \\(Z_\\infty\\) be the completed full marking tower. Completion uses spectral norms on relatively compact analytic charts.</p><p class=\"li\">On the relative Fargues–Fontaine curve put\n\\[\nV_j=\\mathcal O(1/j),\\quad H_j=H^0(V_j),\\quad\nN_m=H^1(V_m^\\vee).\n\\]\nHere \\(V_j\\) has rank \\(j\\), degree \\(1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\((N_m^r)_{\\mathrm{ind}}\\) denote fiberwise \\(\\mathbb Q_p\\)-linearly independent tuples. Additively,\n\\[\nN_m\\cong\\mathbb G_{a,\\mathbb C_p}^{\\diamond}/F_m,\n\\]\nwhere \\(F_m/\\mathbb Q_p\\) is unramified of degree \\(m\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>After a fixed determinant-height normalization,\n\\[\n[Z_\\infty/G]\\cong(N_m^r)_{\\mathrm{ind}},\n\\]\nand \\(Z_\\infty\\to(N_m^r)_{\\mathrm{ind}}\\) is a \\(P_n\\)-torsor. The comparison is relative, commutes with base change and both marking actions, and sends \\((a,b)\\in P_m\\times\\operatorname{GL}_r(\\mathbb Z_p)\\) to pullback along \\(a^{-1}\\) on \\(V_m\\) and pushout along \\(b\\) on \\(\\mathcal O^r\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>An independent tuple of sections defines \\(\\mathcal O^r\\to V_n\\). Over a geometric curve, saturate its generic span to \\(W\\). Since \\(W\\) is generically generated, it has no negative-slope quotient. Stability gives \\(\\deg W&lt;\\operatorname{rk}(W)/n&lt;1\\), so \\(\\deg W=0\\), and classification gives \\(W\\cong\\mathcal O^b\\). Independence forces \\(b=r\\). Thus it is a subbundle.</p><p>Relatively, geometric base curves detect points of the Fargues–Fontaine curve. Surjectivity of the dual on every geometric curve therefore makes an appropriate maximal minor a local unit. Consequently\n\\[\n0\\longrightarrow\\mathcal O^r\\longrightarrow V_n\\longrightarrow Q\\longrightarrow0\n\\]\nis relatively exact with locally free quotient. Every geometric slope of \\(Q\\) is positive; rank \\(m\\) and degree \\(1\\) force \\(Q\\) to have type \\(V_m\\). Relative constant-type classification supplies local frames.</p><p>Conversely, for\n\\[\n0\\longrightarrow\\mathcal O^r\\longrightarrow E\\longrightarrow V_m\\longrightarrow0,\n\\]\nthe connecting map\n\\[\n\\mathbb Q_p^r\\longrightarrow\\operatorname{Ext}^1(V_m,\\mathcal O)=N_m\n\\]\nsends \\((a_i)\\) to \\(\\sum_i a_i\\xi_i\\). The bundle \\(E\\) has a quotient \\(\\mathcal O\\) exactly when this map has nonzero kernel. Thus \\(E\\) has type \\(V_n\\) exactly for independent extension tuples. Since \\(\\operatorname{Hom}(V_m,\\mathcal O^r)=0\\), the extension sheaf has no residual automorphisms fixing both ends.</p><p>The connected marking, whose coefficients are bounded by its monic equations, induces a convergent map of universal-cover sheaves \\(H_n\\to H_m\\). It is linear over continuous \\(\\mathbb Q_p\\)-valued functions by the clopen-approximation argument of Lemma \\(8.1\\). It kills the integral division sections and their rational span, so factors through \\(H^0(Q)\\). Relative full faithfulness produces \\(Q\\to V_m\\). Its nonzero linear marking coefficient makes it nonzero on every geometric fiber; since the stable endomorphism algebra is a division algebra, it is an isomorphism.</p><p>Determinants give a locally constant frame height. The reduced-norm valuation\n\\[\nD_m^\\times\\to\\mathbb Z\n\\]\nis surjective with kernel \\(P_m\\). The height is unchanged by both integral marking groups, hence descends to connected \\(X_m\\) and is constant. Normalize that constant to zero. The equivariant map from connected markings to quotient frames of this height is an isomorphism of \\(P_m\\)-torsors.</p><p>Finally, for every independent extension, middle frames of the determinant-prescribed height form a locally nonempty \\(P_n\\)-torsor. Lemma \\(8.2\\) identifies these with \\(Z_\\infty\\). Taking the quotient proves the statement. Postcomposition of a quotient frame by \\(a\\) gives pullback along \\(a^{-1}\\); changing the kernel frame by \\(b\\) gives pushout along \\(b\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.1", "orig": "Lemma \\(4.1\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Its universal-cover sheaf identifies covariantly with \\(H_j\\), preserving composition of rational Honda endomorphisms.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the Honda model with \\(p\\)-series \\(T^{p^j}\\), and the arithmetic-Frobenius convention.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Its universal-cover sheaf identifies covariantly with \\(H_j\\), preserving composition of rational Honda endomorphisms. Geometrically,\n\\[\nD_j=\\operatorname{End}(V_j)\\cong\\operatorname{End}^0(\\Gamma_j),\n\\qquad\nP_j=\\mathcal O_{D_j}^{\\times}.\n\\]\nThe comparison is relative and linear over the sheaf of continuous \\(\\mathbb Q_p\\)-valued functions.</p>", "proof": "<p>For a topologically nilpotent coordinate \\(X\\), the covariant section map is\n\\[\n\\psi_j(X)=\\sum_{i\\in\\mathbb Z}p^i[X^{p^{-ji}}],\n\\qquad\n\\varphi^j\\psi_j(X)=p\\psi_j(X).\n\\]\nThe section comparison of Fargues–Scholze, pushed forward along the unramified degree-\\(j\\) coefficient-field map, identifies its target with \\(H_j\\).</p><p>On a closed period annulus, with exponent parameter in \\([a,b]\\), \\(a&gt;0\\), and \\(\\|X\\|\\leq\\rho&lt;1\\), the \\(i\\)-th term is bounded by\n\\[\np^{-i}\\rho^{ap^{-ji}}.\n\\]\nBoth tails tend uniformly to zero. Thus the formula is continuous and compatible with bounded base change.</p><p>Termwise calculation gives\n\\[\n\\psi_j(X^p)=\\varphi\\psi_j(X),\\qquad\n\\psi_j(cX)=[c]\\psi_j(X),\n\\]\nand the Honda relations\n\\[\n\\Pi_j^j=p,\\qquad \\Pi_j[c]=[c^p]\\Pi_j\n\\]\nhold with the same composition on both sides. Continuity extends compatibility from integers to \\(\\mathbb Z_p\\), and then rationally.</p><p>For a varying scalar \\(\\alpha\\), choose \\(d\\) with \\(b=p^d\\alpha\\) integral, approximate \\(b\\) by clopen-constant \\(b_h\\) modulo \\(p^h\\), and use\n\\[\n\\|[b]v-_{\\Gamma_j}[b_h]v\\|\\leq\\rho^{p^{jh}},\n\\qquad\n\\|(b-b_h)\\psi_j(v)\\|\\leq|p|^h\\|\\psi_j(v)\\|.\n\\]\nTaking limits proves sheaf \\(\\mathbb Q_p\\)-linearity.</p><p>Positive-slope vanishing and Anschütz–Le Bras relative full faithfulness turn the action into a composition-preserving map of endomorphism algebras. It is injective because the source is a division algebra; both geometric algebras have dimension \\(j^2\\), so it is an isomorphism. Their unique maximal orders correspond.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.2", "orig": "Lemma \\(4.3\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is a relative \\(G\\times\\operatorname{GL}_r(\\mathbb Z_p)\\)-equivariant analytic isomorphism \\( Z_\\infty^{\\mathrm{et}}\\cong(H_n^r)_{\\mathrm{ind}}, \\) obtained by restricting an isomorphism between the completed …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(Z_\\infty^{\\mathrm{et}}\\) be the completed perfected tower with only ordered étale Tate-module markings.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a relative \\(G\\times\\operatorname{GL}_r(\\mathbb Z_p)\\)-equivariant analytic isomorphism\n\\[\nZ_\\infty^{\\mathrm{et}}\\cong(H_n^r)_{\\mathrm{ind}},\n\\]\nobtained by restricting an isomorphism between the completed perfected tower of reduced closures and the open ball underlying \\(H_n^r\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(q_n=p^n\\), \\(q_m=p^m\\). On a bounded chart with parameter norm at most \\(\\lambda&lt;1\\),\n\\[\n[p]_{\\mathcal F}(T)\\equiv T^{q_n}\\pmod{(u_m,\\ldots,u_{n-1})},\n\\qquad\n[p]_{\\mathcal F}(T)\\in A_m[[T^{q_m}]],\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n|[p]_{\\mathcal F}(s)-s^{q_n}|\\leq\\lambda,\\qquad\n|[p]_{\\mathcal F}(s)-[p]_{\\mathcal F}(t)|\\leq|s-t|^{q_m}.\n\\]\nFor a compatible division sequence, define\n\\[\nz=\\lim_{j\\to\\infty}t_j^{q_n^j}.\n\\]\nConversely,\n\\[\nt_j=\\lim_{N\\to\\infty}[p]^N_{\\mathcal F}\n\\left(z^{1/q_n^{j+N}}\\right).\n\\]\nThe successive errors are bounded by \\(\\lambda^{q_n^j}\\) and \\(\\lambda^{q_m^N}\\), respectively, proving uniform convergence and inverse identities.</p><p>Proposition \\(5\\) gives free coordinates \\(t_{i,\\ell}\\), base expressions in \\(t_{i,1}^{q_m}\\), and earlier coordinates in \\(t_{i,\\ell}^{q_m^{\\ell-j}}\\). On\n\\[\nX_\\ell(\\rho)=\\{\\max_i|t_{i,\\ell}^{q_n^\\ell}|\\leq\\rho\\},\n\\]\none obtains\n\\[\n\\lambda\\leq\\rho^{q_m/q_n},\n\\qquad\n\\|t_{\\cdot,\\ell+1}^{q_n^{\\ell+1}}-\nt_{\\cdot,\\ell}^{q_n^\\ell}\\|\n\\leq\\rho^{q_mq_n^{\\ell-1}}.\n\\]\nThe finite transition maps are surjective on these charts, hence isometric on spectral norms.</p><p>The limits \\(z_i\\) induce an isometric map from the perfected radius-\\(\\rho\\) Gauss algebra into the completed direct limit: polynomial norms agree at every sufficiently late free-coordinate level. Its image is closed. Substituting \\(z_i^{1/q_n^\\ell}\\) in the earlier-coordinate formulas has error tending to zero like \\(\\lambda^{q_m^{\\ell-j}}\\); hence all finite-level generators, and their roots, lie in the image. They are dense, proving surjectivity.</p><p>The estimates are uniform under bounded base change and compact parameters. Replacing deformation addition, scalar operations, or group coordinate changes by Honda reductions introduces errors contracted by the same estimates, proving equivariance.</p><p>A rational relation among normalized sections is equivalent to the relation among compatible division sequences. On exact height \\(m\\), the marked étale module has rank \\(r\\); on deeper height it has smaller rank. This identifies the independent open.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 9", "orig": "Lemma \\(4.4\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The compact torsors \\( Z_U\\longleftarrow Z_\\infty\\longrightarrow(N_m^r)_{\\mathrm{ind}}, \\) with groups \\(U,G\\), transport support systems cofinally, and …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use Proposition \\(8\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Compact supports are filtered colimits of restriction fibers away from relatively compact quasicompact bounds, interleaving closed boxes with slightly larger open boxes.</p><p class=\"li\">On \\(X_m\\), use boxes\n\\[\n\\epsilon\\leq|x|\\leq\\rho&lt;1,\\qquad |y_i|\\leq\\rho.\n\\]\nBounds are uniform over retained compact profinite parameter spaces \\(D\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The compact torsors\n\\[\nZ_U\\longleftarrow Z_\\infty\\longrightarrow(N_m^r)_{\\mathrm{ind}},\n\\]\nwith groups \\(U,G\\), transport support systems cofinally, and\n\\[\nR\\Gamma\\!\\left(G,R\\Gamma_c(Z_U,\\mathcal O^\\flat)\\right)\n\\simeq\nR\\Gamma\\!\\left(U,R\\Gamma_c((N_m^r)_{\\mathrm{ind}},\\mathcal O^\\flat)\\right).\n\\]\nThis includes uniform compact bar parameters.</p>", "proof": "<p>A profinite-group torsor is a limit of finite étale torsors. Over an affinoid perfectoid test space these are affinoid, and the limit is affinoid perfectoid and quasicompact. Thus quasicompact bounds have quasicompact inverse images. Conversely their images are quasicompact and lie in a larger box from the increasing slightly-open exhaustion. The relative isomorphism of Proposition \\(8\\) preserves these cofinal systems.</p><p>Saturating a bound under compact \\(G\\times U\\) remains quasicompact and fits in a larger bound. On invariant bounds, torsor Čech nerves are continuous bar constructions, including supports. The commuting actions give the same double bar complex whichever descent is taken first. The complexes are bounded below, so each total degree uses finitely many degrees; filtered support colimits commute with the resulting finite sums and fibers. The same argument retains compact parameters.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 10", "orig": "Proposition \\(5.2\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The Kummer tower computes \\( R\\Gamma(A_S[a,b],\\mathcal O^\\flat) \\simeq [M_R[a,b]\\xrightarrow{\\gamma-1}M_R[a,b]], \\qquad \\gamma(T^u)=\\varepsilon^uT^u. \\) If …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(S=\\operatorname{Spa}(R,R^+)\\) be affinoid perfectoid over \\(\\mathbb C_p^\\flat\\), with specified untilt, and let\n\\[\nA_S=(\\mathbb A^1_{\\mathbb C_p})^\\diamond\\times_{\\operatorname{Spd}\\mathbb C_p}S.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">The sheaf \\(\\mathcal O^\\flat\\) is the tilted structural sheaf on perfectoid test objects, not the structural sheaf of the perfected characteristic-\\(p\\) line.</p><p class=\"li\">Choose compatible roots of unity and their tilt \\(\\varepsilon\\), with\n\\[\n\\kappa=-\\log|\\varepsilon-1|&gt;0.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(0&lt;a&lt;b\\), define\n\\[\nM_R[a,b]\n=\n\\left\\{\n\\sum_{u\\in\\mathbb Z[1/p]}c_uT^u:\n\\|c_u\\|\\max(a^u,b^u)\\to0\n\\right\\}.\n\\]\nDecay means that only finitely many terms exceed any positive norm threshold.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The Kummer tower computes\n\\[\nR\\Gamma(A_S[a,b],\\mathcal O^\\flat)\n\\simeq\n[M_R[a,b]\\xrightarrow{\\gamma-1}M_R[a,b]],\n\\qquad\n\\gamma(T^u)=\\varepsilon^uT^u.\n\\]\nIf\n\\[\na&lt;a'&lt;b'&lt;b,\\qquad a'/a\\geq e^\\kappa,\\qquad b/b'\\geq e^\\kappa,\n\\]\nrestriction is chain homotopic to constant-monomial projection and factors through \\(R[0]\\oplus R[-1]\\). The homotopy is bounded, \\(R\\)-linear, uniform in compact profinite parameters, and compatible with base change and translation.</p>", "proof": "<p>Adjoining all \\(p\\)-power roots of \\(T\\) gives a perfectoid \\(\\mathbb Z_p(1)\\)-torsor. Its tilt has the displayed Laurent algebra, and its nerve has continuous-function rings on compact powers of \\(\\mathbb Z_p(1)\\). Perfectoid \\(\\mathcal O^\\flat\\)-acyclicity and Proposition \\(1\\) give the two-term complex.</p><p>For \\(u=p^vm\\neq0\\), \\(m\\) prime to \\(p\\),\n\\[\n|\\varepsilon^u-1|=|\\varepsilon-1|^{p^v},\n\\qquad\n|(\\varepsilon^u-1)^{-1}|\\leq e^{\\kappa|u|_{\\mathbb R}}.\n\\]\nDefine on degree one\n\\[\nH\\!\\left(\\sum_uc_uT^u\\right)\n=\n\\sum_{u\\neq0}\\frac{c_u}{\\varepsilon^u-1}T^u.\n\\]\nFor positive exponents the radius change contributes \\((b'/b)^u\\leq e^{-\\kappa u}\\); for negative ones it contributes \\((a'/a)^u\\leq e^{-\\kappa|u|}\\). Thus \\(H\\) has norm at most one, including for unbounded root denominators, and\n\\[\n(\\gamma-1)H+H(\\gamma-1)=\\mathrm{res}-P_0.\n\\]\nThe coefficients of this operator are fixed scalars, proving all uniformity assertions. This is a comparison between separated annuli, not a finite-dimensionality assertion for a fixed annulus.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.1", "orig": "Lemma \\(5.1\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( R^1\\varprojlim_jE_j=0. \\) The same holds for \\(C(D,E_j)\\), with supremum norm, for compact profinite \\(D\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\((E_j,f_j)\\) be a countable inverse system of complete nonarchimedean normed vector spaces with dense continuous transition maps.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nR^1\\varprojlim_jE_j=0.\n\\]\nThe same holds for \\(C(D,E_j)\\), with supremum norm, for compact profinite \\(D\\). A system of complexes satisfying this degreewise is computed by its termwise inverse limit.</p>", "proof": "<p>The derived limit is represented by\n\\[\n\\prod_jE_j\\longrightarrow\\prod_jE_j,\n\\qquad\n(v_j)\\longmapsto(v_j-f_j(v_{j+1})).\n\\]\nGiven a target \\((a_j)\\), construct finite solution strings. At the next stage use density to make\n\\[\na_h+f_h(v_{h+1})-v_h\n\\]\narbitrarily small. Recompute earlier terms so all equations remain true, making their finitely many changes at most \\(2^{-h}\\). Completeness gives a limiting solution.</p><p>Continuous parameter functions are uniformly approximated by finite clopen-constant functions; lift their finitely many values approximately. Thus the parameter transition maps also have dense image. Degreewise surjectivity of the product map identifies the derived limit with its kernel complex.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 11", "orig": "Proposition \\(5.4\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is \\(A_p>1\\) such that restriction from a disc of radius \\(r_{\\mathrm{out}}\\) to a concentric disc of radius \\(r\\), for \\(r_{\\mathrm{out}}/r\\geq A_p\\), factors through evaluation \\(R[0]\\) and constant inclusion.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the relative untilted affine line and coefficients of Proposition \\(10\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n\\[\nR\\Gamma_K(X,\\mathcal E)\n=\n\\operatorname{fib}\\bigl(R\\Gamma(X,\\mathcal E)\\to R\\Gamma(X\\setminus K,\\mathcal E)\\bigr).\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Compact supports are colimits over interleaved relatively compact bounds.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p class=\"li num\">1. There is \\(A_p&gt;1\\) such that restriction from a disc of radius \\(r_{\\mathrm{out}}\\) to a concentric disc of radius \\(r\\), for \\(r_{\\mathrm{out}}/r\\geq A_p\\), factors through evaluation \\(R[0]\\) and constant inclusion. Every fixed disc has \\(H^0=R\\), and its shrinking section germ is \\(R[0]\\).\n2.\n\\[\nR\\Gamma(A_S,\\mathcal O^\\flat)\\simeq R[0],\n\\qquad\nR\\Gamma_c(A_S,\\mathcal O^\\flat)\\simeq R[-2].\n\\]\nAfter a fixed support-radius enlargement, the support transition has pure degree-two image and identifies that image with final compact-support cohomology; the cone of the comparison to \\(R[-2]\\) maps cohomologically to zero.</p><p class=\"li num\">3. For \\(d\\) ordered affine coordinates, ordinary and section-germ complexes are \\(R[0]\\), and box compact supports are \\(R[-2d]\\), with the corresponding uniform pure-image comparison.</p><p class=\"li num\">4. With \\(\\mathbb F_p\\)-coefficients, replace \\(R\\) by \\(R\\Gamma(S,\\mathbb F_p)\\). For \\(S=\\operatorname{Spa}(\\mathbb C_p^\\flat)\\times D\\), this is \\(C(D,\\mathbb F_p)[0]\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">5. All statements are relative and parameter-uniform. Support orientation is translation-invariant, and finitely many disjoint-disc classes add under inclusion into a larger support disc.</p>", "proof": "<p>Translate the section to zero. Put \\(\\tau_p=|p|^{p/(p-1)}\\). Choose a scalar \\(\\alpha\\) of norm \\(s\\), with \\(r/s&lt;\\tau_p\\), and nested annuli centered at \\(\\alpha\\), both containing the small disc, separated as in Proposition \\(10\\), and lying in the large disc. The disc restriction factors through \\(R[0]\\oplus R[-1]\\).</p><p>On the small disc,\n\\[\nT-\\alpha=-\\alpha(1-T/\\alpha)\n\\]\nhas an analytic \\(p\\)-th root by the binomial series. Comparing this root with the first Kummer root gives a locally constant \\(b\\) satisfying \\((\\gamma-1)b=1\\). Hence the degree-one constant Kummer class is nullhomotopic, leaving evaluation.</p><p>A fixed disc is covered by two annuli omitting disjoint small discs. Each annular degree-zero kernel is \\(R\\); their overlap contains a constant section, so the constants agree. Thus \\(H^0=R\\). The shrinking restriction system has no positive-degree colimit.</p><p>Exhaust the line by discs with successive ratios at least \\(A_p\\). Degree-zero cohomology is constant \\(R\\), and positive-degree systems are pro-zero. The countable Milnor exact sequence gives ordinary cohomology \\(R[0]\\).</p><p>For \\(E_S(r)=\\{|T|&gt;r\\}\\), annular exhaustion and Lemma \\(10.1\\) give the same two-term Kummer complex on Laurent series convergent on every closed exterior annulus. Increasing the inner radius absorbs negative-exponent denominators; positive-exponent denominators are absorbed by choosing a larger available outer seminorm. Thus\n\\[\n\\operatorname*{colim}_{r\\to\\infty}R\\Gamma(E_S(r),\\mathcal O^\\flat)\n\\simeq R[0]\\oplus R[-1].\n\\]\nThe line maps identically onto the first summand, so its fiber is \\(R[-2]\\). The annular homotopy preserves constant inclusion; taking its fiber gives the fixed-radius pure-image statement before the support colimit.</p><p>The boundary of the exterior Kummer cocycle of \\(T-c\\) is the chosen support generator. For two bounded centers, \\((T-c')/(T-c)\\) has a \\(p\\)-th root on a sufficiently large exterior. This proves translation invariance. Disjoint support excision gives addition of classes.</p><p>Iterating the actual box restriction fibers in ordered coordinates gives \\(R[-2d]\\); evaluation gives \\(R[0]\\). The same radius losses work in each coordinate.</p><p>Finally apply\n\\[\n0\\to\\mathbb F_p\\to\\mathcal O^\\flat\n\\xrightarrow{\\mathrm{Fr}-1}\\mathcal O^\\flat\\to0.\n\\]\nThe Laurent homotopies commute with Frobenius because\n\\[\n(\\varepsilon^u-1)^p=\\varepsilon^{pu}-1.\n\\]\nTaking the fiber gives the base \\(\\mathbb F_p\\)-complex with the same shifts. For \\(C(D,\\mathbb C_p^\\flat)\\), Artin–Schreier has continuous local solution branches; a finite clopen refinement of \\(D\\) gives surjectivity and kernel \\(C(D,\\mathbb F_p)\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 12", "orig": "Lemma \\(5.6\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For \\(\\mathcal E=\\mathcal O^\\flat\\) or \\(\\mathbb F_p\\), put \\( E_j=\\operatorname{fib}\\bigl(R\\Gamma(X,\\mathcal E) \\to R\\Gamma(X\\setminus T_j,\\mathcal E)\\bigr). \\) The inverse systems \\(H^0(E_j)\\) and \\(H^1(E_j)\\) are …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(X\\) be a bounded relative untilted affine chart over a base \\(v\\)-sheaf, with a slightly enlarged chart for collars, and \\(Z\\subseteq X\\) closed.</p><p class=\"li\">After finite quasicompact base refinement, assume a cofinal decreasing neighborhood sequence \\(T_j\\) consists of finite disjoint unions of relative discs or boxes in at least one normal coordinate, centered at actual analytic sections.</p><p class=\"li\">Assume child clusters refine parents and a fixed further depth \\(N\\) supplies the radius ratio of Proposition \\(11\\), uniformly in \\(j\\), compact parameters, and the finitely many support collars.</p><p class=\"li\">Assume separated outer collars, \\(\\bigcap_jT_j=Z\\), and an admissible exhaustion of \\(X\\setminus Z\\) by their complements.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For \\(\\mathcal E=\\mathcal O^\\flat\\) or \\(\\mathbb F_p\\), put\n\\[\nE_j=\\operatorname{fib}\\bigl(R\\Gamma(X,\\mathcal E)\n\\to R\\Gamma(X\\setminus T_j,\\mathcal E)\\bigr).\n\\]\nThe inverse systems \\(H^0(E_j)\\) and \\(H^1(E_j)\\) are pro-zero. Consequently\n\\[\nH^0R\\Gamma_Z(X,\\mathcal E)=H^1R\\Gamma_Z(X,\\mathcal E)=0,\n\\]\nand deleting \\(Z\\) preserves relative degree-zero sections.</p><p>The conclusion survives bounded-below descent with finite required refinements in each fixed total degree. It also holds on a compatible countable exhaustion by such bounds, even when tube refinements depend on the bound.</p>", "proof": "<p>Collar excision identifies \\(E_{j+N}\\to E_j\\) with a finite sum of child-to-parent support maps. Proposition \\(11\\) makes each such map have pure geometric image in degree at least two. The estimate holds before the finite sum. For \\(\\mathbb F_p\\), the retained base complex is connective, so the same low-degree vanishing holds.</p><p>In a fixed total descent degree, only finitely many cosimplicial degrees can contribute. Make their chart and collar refinements simultaneously. A map zero on associated graded pieces lowers a finite filtration; composing finitely many further refinements kills the whole low-degree map.</p><p>The complements exhaust \\(X\\setminus Z\\), so\n\\[\nR\\Gamma_Z(X,\\mathcal E)\\simeq R\\varprojlim_jE_j.\n\\]\nThe complexes are connective. The Milnor sequence and pro-zero \\(H^0,H^1\\), including \\(\\varprojlim^1H^0=0\\), give the asserted vanishing.</p><p>For an unbounded exhaustion \\(X_a\\), first form the canonical support complexes\n\\[\nC_a=R\\Gamma_{Z\\cap X_a}(X_a,\\mathcal E).\n\\]\nEach is connective with \\(H^0=H^1=0\\). Their derived inverse limit computes the global support fiber, and the same Milnor sequence proves the result. No common tube depth across distinct output bounds is required.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 13", "orig": "Proposition \\(6.2\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For \\(s\\geq0\\), \\( R\\Gamma_c(N_m^s\\times D,\\mathbb F_p) \\simeq E_D[-(m+2)s], \\) \\( R\\Gamma_c(N_m^s\\times D,\\mathcal O^\\flat) \\simeq R_D[-(m+2)s]. \\) Extension of constants identifies these comparisons, compatibly with …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(F_m/\\mathbb Q_p\\) be unramified of degree \\(m\\), embedded in \\(\\mathbb C_p\\), and put\n\\[\nN_m=\\mathbb G_{a,\\mathbb C_p}^{\\diamond}/F_m.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">For compact profinite \\(D\\), put\n\\[\nE_D=C(D,\\mathbb F_p),\\qquad R_D=C(D,\\mathbb C_p^\\flat).\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Supports are uniform in \\(D\\), and geometric Kummer orientations are chosen.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For \\(s\\geq0\\),\n\\[\nR\\Gamma_c(N_m^s\\times D,\\mathbb F_p)\n\\simeq E_D[-(m+2)s],\n\\]\n\\[\nR\\Gamma_c(N_m^s\\times D,\\mathcal O^\\flat)\n\\simeq R_D[-(m+2)s].\n\\]\nExtension of constants identifies these comparisons, compatibly with compact continuous families of automorphisms and the support and lattice maps.</p>", "proof": "<p>For one coordinate, choose discs \\(D_a\\) of radius \\(R_0|p|^{-a}\\), lattices\n\\[\nL_a=\\{b\\in F_m:|b|\\leq R_0|p|^{-a}\\},\n\\]\nand quotient bounds \\(B_a\\). Interleave radii so \\(L_{a+1}=p^{-1}L_a\\). These bounds are cofinal.</p><p>The inverse image of \\(B_a\\) is the locally finite disjoint union\n\\[\n\\coprod_{b\\in F_m/L_a}(D_a+b).\n\\]\nSections with that support are the product over the translates, hence continuously coinduced from \\(L_a\\). Translation descent and explicit bar Shapiro identify the quotient-support complex with\n\\[\nR\\Gamma\\!\\left(L_a,R\\Gamma_{D_a}(\\mathbb G_a^\\diamond,\\mathcal E)\\right).\n\\]</p><p>Proposition \\(11\\) compares the geometric support to the oriented base complex shifted by two, with cohomologically zero cone after fixed enlargement. Enlarge geometry first while retaining the smaller lattice; only then group finitely many translated supports. This preserves the cone vanishing and makes the oriented maps corestriction. In each fixed total degree, bounded-below spectral sequences involve finitely many bar degrees, so a common enlargement suffices. Thus\n\\[\nR\\Gamma_c(N_m,\\mathcal E)\n\\simeq\n\\operatorname*{colim}_{a,\\mathrm{cor}}R\\Gamma(L_a,\\mathcal E_{\\mathrm{base}})[-2].\n\\]\nLemma \\(13.1\\) retains only lattice degree \\(m\\).</p><p>For \\(s\\) coordinates use cofinal product boxes and the rank-\\(ms\\) product lattice. The geometric degree is \\(2s\\), and the surviving lattice degree is \\(ms\\). All constructions retain uniform compact parameters and canonical support maps.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 13.1", "orig": "Lemma \\(6.1\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Continuous cohomology is the exterior algebra on \\(\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathrm{cts}}(L,\\mathbb F_p)\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(L\\) be a rank-\\(e\\) lattice in a finite-dimensional \\(\\mathbb Q_p\\)-space, and let \\(L'=p^{-1}L\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Coefficients are trivial of characteristic \\(p\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Continuous cohomology is the exterior algebra on \\(\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathrm{cts}}(L,\\mathbb F_p)\\). Restriction \\(L'\\to L\\) is the identity in degree zero and zero in positive degrees. Corestriction \\(L\\to L'\\) is zero below degree \\(e\\) and identifies top orientation lines. These assertions persist with \\(R_D\\) or relative base complexes.</p>", "proof": "<p>Use the completed Koszul resolution on a lattice basis. Over \\(\\mathbb Z_p\\), restriction in degree \\(i\\) multiplies by \\(p^i\\). The identity \\(\\mathrm{res}\\,\\mathrm{cor}=p^e\\), and torsion-freeness, give corestriction \\(p^{e-i}\\). Reduce modulo \\(p\\). The same finite complexes tensored with the retained coefficients give the relative statements.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 14", "orig": "Theorem \\(6.5\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There are natural quasi-isomorphisms with algebraic tensors \\( E_D\\otimes_{\\mathbb F_p}R\\Gamma_c(Y_s,\\mathbb F_p) \\simeq R\\Gamma_c(Y_s\\times D,\\mathbb F_p), \\) …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Put\n\\[\nY_s=(N_m^s)_{\\mathrm{ind}},\\qquad Y_0=\\operatorname{Spa}(\\mathbb C_p^\\flat),\n\\qquad M_s=\\operatorname{GL}_s(\\mathbb Z_p).\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Use \\(E_D,R_D\\) and uniform supports from Proposition \\(13\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are natural quasi-isomorphisms with algebraic tensors\n\\[\nE_D\\otimes_{\\mathbb F_p}R\\Gamma_c(Y_s,\\mathbb F_p)\n\\simeq R\\Gamma_c(Y_s\\times D,\\mathbb F_p),\n\\]\n\\[\nR_D\\otimes_{\\mathbb F_p}R\\Gamma_c(Y_s,\\mathbb F_p)\n\\simeq R\\Gamma_c(Y_s\\times D,\\mathcal O^\\flat).\n\\]\nThey retain uniform support and compact bar parameters.</p><p>Each \\(H_c^i(Y_s,\\mathbb F_p)\\) is countable, smooth, and admissible for \\(M_s\\), with a smooth commuting \\(P_m\\)-action.</p>", "proof": "<p>Induct on \\(s\\). The whole-space comparison is Proposition \\(13\\). Delete ranks \\(0,\\ldots,s-1\\) using Lemma \\(14.2\\). Its germ terms have the desired comparison by induction, since \\(\\mathrm{LC}(P,-)\\) is an exact colimit of finite sums and commutes with algebraic scalar extension.</p><p>Integral coefficient quotients have saturated lattice kernels; hence \\(M_s\\) acts transitively on the coefficient Grassmannian and integral graph charts have continuous local sections. Changes of trivialization act through \\(\\operatorname{GL}_t(\\mathbb Z_p)\\); inductive smoothness makes their action on any finite set of classes locally constant. Finite clopen refinements glue the comparison without losing finite-span structure.</p><p>For a compact acting group \\(H\\), pullback of a class along the action is, by the parameter comparison, an element of\n\\[\nC(H,\\mathbb F_p)\\otimes H_c^i(Y_s,\\mathbb F_p).\n\\]\nIts orbit map is locally constant and finite-valued, proving smoothness for both commuting actions.</p><p>If \\(\\Pi_t\\subseteq M_s\\) stabilizes the standard coefficient plane, then\n\\[\nH^iG_{s,t}(\\mathbb F_p)\n\\cong\n\\operatorname{Ind}_{\\Pi_t}^{M_s}H_c^i(Y_t,\\mathbb F_p).\n\\]\nThe other Levi factor and unipotent radical act trivially on the normal degree-zero germ. An open subgroup has finitely many orbits on \\(M_s/\\Pi_t\\), and each stabilizer maps onto an open subgroup in the \\(\\operatorname{GL}_t\\)-factor. Thus induction preserves admissibility.</p><p>Admissible smooth modules for a compact analytic group are closed under subobjects, quotients, and extensions: their compact duals are finitely generated over the Noetherian completed group algebra, by compact Nakayama. Apply this closure to the support triangles. Countability follows from the countable clopen basis of the compact Grassmannian and induction.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 14.1", "orig": "Proposition \\(6.3\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "After finite quasicompact refinement there exist \\(0<\\alpha<\\beta\\) and a fixed lattice \\(L\\subseteq\\mathbb Q_p^t\\oplus F_m\\) such that …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">A bounded quasicompact perfectoid base chart carries untilted lifts \\(z_1,\\ldots,z_t\\) of independent classes in \\(N_m\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a compact open slope patch in \\(\\mathbb Q_p^t\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>After finite quasicompact refinement there exist \\(0&lt;\\alpha&lt;\\beta\\) and a fixed lattice \\(L\\subseteq\\mathbb Q_p^t\\oplus F_m\\) such that\n\\[\nL=\\{w:|\\ell_z(w)|\\leq\\alpha\\}\n=\\{w:|\\ell_z(w)|&lt;\\beta\\},\n\\quad\n\\ell_z(a,b)=\\sum_i a_iz_i+b.\n\\]\nAt every depth \\(j\\), the same chart has lattice \\(p^jL\\) for radii \\(\\alpha|p|^j,\\beta|p|^j\\).</p><p>Writing \\(L_a=\\operatorname{pr}_{\\mathbb Q_p^t}L\\), \\(L_b=L\\cap F_m\\), sufficiently deep slope clusters are \\(p^jL_a\\)-cosets. Their graph tubes are disjoint quotient discs centered at actual sections and stabilized by \\(p^jL_b\\). Closed and open tubes are interleaved and neighborhood-cofinal, including collars and compact parameters. Finite products admit simultaneous refinements.</p>", "proof": "<p>Independence makes \\(\\ell_z\\) injective on each geometric fiber. Compactness of the unit sphere and finite-net estimates give, after finite base refinement,\n\\[\nc\\|w\\|\\leq|\\ell_z(w)|\\leq M\\|w\\|,\n\\qquad c&gt;0.\n\\]\nOn a compact coefficient annulus, the nonzero nonarchimedean norm takes finitely many values. Choose \\(\\alpha&lt;\\beta\\) in a gap. A finite coefficient net makes membership in the corresponding lattice persist on an open base neighborhood. Scaling follows from \\(\\ell_z(p^jw)=p^j\\ell_z(w)\\).</p><p>The exact sequence \\(0\\to L_b\\to L\\to L_a\\to0\\) splits over \\(\\mathbb Z_p\\). Fixing a splitting gives continuous translation choices within slope clusters. Distinct quotient tubes intersect only if their coefficient difference lies in \\(p^jL\\), hence only within one slope cluster. Their stabilizer is \\(p^jL_b\\).</p><p>Refined representatives can be translated into parent discs. Ultrametricity allows the parent disc to be recentered at a child, so the comparisons are the concentric maps of Proposition \\(11\\). A fixed number of depths supplies its radius ratio.</p><p>A rational neighborhood of an analytic section contains a disc of some positive radius after quasicompact refinement, by denominator lower bounds and Taylor estimates. Apply this to the compact graph family and choose a finite refinement and a common radius. This proves cofinality; the lower estimate proves that the intersection is the graph. Slightly enlarged independent bounds retain the lower estimate and give compatible collars. Compact parameters and finite products are handled by common finite refinements.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 14.2", "orig": "Lemma \\(6.4\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is a natural triangle \\( R\\Gamma_c(X_{s,\\geq t+1},\\mathcal E) \\to R\\Gamma_c(X_{s,\\geq t},\\mathcal E) \\to G_{s,t}(\\mathcal E)\\to, \\) where \\(G_{s,t}\\) uses shrinking neighborhood germs with compact support along …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(X_{s,\\geq t}\\subseteq N_m^s\\) be the locus of rank at least \\(t\\), and \\(Z_{s,t}\\) its closed rank-\\(t\\) locus.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal E=\\mathbb F_p\\) or \\(\\mathcal O^\\flat\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For a compact open Grassmannian graph patch \\(P\\), let \\(\\mathrm{LC}(P,-)\\) be the algebraic colimit of finite direct sums over finite clopen partitions.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a natural triangle\n\\[\nR\\Gamma_c(X_{s,\\geq t+1},\\mathcal E)\n\\to R\\Gamma_c(X_{s,\\geq t},\\mathcal E)\n\\to G_{s,t}(\\mathcal E)\\to,\n\\]\nwhere \\(G_{s,t}\\) uses shrinking neighborhood germs with compact support along the stratum. Locally on \\(P\\),\n\\[\nG_{s,t}(\\mathcal E)\n\\simeq\n\\mathrm{LC}\\bigl(P,R\\Gamma_c(Y_t,\\mathcal E)\\bigr),\n\\]\nand with external \\(D\\), replace \\(Y_t\\) by \\(Y_t\\times D\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>On a fixed bound and collar, restrict supported sections to shrinking neighborhoods of \\(Z_{s,t}\\). The fiber is supported away from the neighborhood. These remaining bounds are cofinal among compact bounds in the complement. Exact filtered colimits give the triangle.</p><p>Use Lemma \\(14.1\\). At depth \\(j\\), there are finitely many actual disc tubes; translation-cover Shapiro reduces to compact stabilizers \\(p^jL_b\\). Pullback of the retained base complex to each tube gives compatible comparison maps. Proposition \\(11\\) kills the geometric cone after a fixed shrinking ratio, uniformly in compact stabilizer-bar parameters in each fixed total degree.</p><p>The group transitions are restriction to smaller lattices. Lemma \\(13.1\\) kills positive lattice degrees and preserves degree zero, copying a base class into every refined slope cluster. Thus the germ is the algebraic locally constant slope family of base complexes.</p><p>Centers are sections on whole refined charts, so comparisons are base-linear and agree on overlaps. Bounded-below descent and support fibers retain this calculation. Finite partitions are used at every stage; no unrestricted infinitely fine slope family is introduced.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 15", "orig": "Corollary \\(6.7\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every integer \\(j\\), \\( H^jR\\Gamma\\!\\left(G,R\\Gamma_c(Z_U,\\mathcal O^\\flat)\\right) \\) is finite-dimensional over \\(\\mathbb C_p^\\flat\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(U\\subseteq P_m\\times\\operatorname{GL}_r(\\mathbb Z_p)\\) be a sufficiently deep product open subgroup, and let \\(Z_U\\) be the analytic marked cover.</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(G=P_n\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every integer \\(j\\),\n\\[\nH^jR\\Gamma\\!\\left(G,R\\Gamma_c(Z_U,\\mathcal O^\\flat)\\right)\n\\]\nis finite-dimensional over \\(\\mathbb C_p^\\flat\\). With retained compact profinite \\(D\\), the groups are scalar extensions to \\(R_D\\), finite over that ring.</p>", "proof": "<p>By Proposition \\(9\\), the complex equals\n\\[\nR\\Gamma\\!\\left(U,R\\Gamma_c(Y_r,\\mathcal O^\\flat)\\right).\n\\]\nWrite \\(U=U_m\\times U_r\\). Proposition \\(14\\) makes geometric cohomology scalar extension of countable smooth admissible \\(M_r\\)-modules. Lemma \\(15.1\\) makes their \\(U_r\\)-cohomology finite in each degree. The residual \\(U_m\\)-action on these finite spaces is smooth; a finite-type resolution gives finite \\(U_m\\)-cohomology.</p><p>The successive first-quadrant spectral sequences have finitely many entries per total degree. Parameter comparisons identify the answer after scalar extension to \\(\\mathbb C_p^\\flat\\) or \\(R_D\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 15.1", "orig": "Lemma \\(6.6\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Every \\(H_{\\mathrm{cts}}^a(H,V)\\) is finite-dimensional, and \\( H_{\\mathrm{cts}}^a(H,R_D\\otimes_{\\mathbb F_p}V) \\cong R_D\\otimes_{\\mathbb F_p}H_{\\mathrm{cts}}^a(H,V). \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(H\\) be compact \\(p\\)-adic analytic and \\(V\\) a countable smooth admissible \\(\\mathbb F_p[H]\\)-module.</p><p class=\"li\">On \\(R_D\\otimes_{\\mathbb F_p}V\\), \\(H\\) acts trivially on \\(R_D\\); coefficients use uniform finite-span complete steps.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every \\(H_{\\mathrm{cts}}^a(H,V)\\) is finite-dimensional, and\n\\[\nH_{\\mathrm{cts}}^a(H,R_D\\otimes_{\\mathbb F_p}V)\n\\cong\nR_D\\otimes_{\\mathbb F_p}H_{\\mathrm{cts}}^a(H,V).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The compact dual \\(V^\\vee\\) is finitely generated over \\(\\mathbb F_p[[H]]\\). Noetherianity gives a resolution with finitely generated free terms. Tensoring with \\(\\mathbb F_p\\) gives finite Tor groups, dual to the required cohomology.</p><p>Exhaust \\(V\\) by finite-dimensional \\(H\\)-stable subspaces. Such an exhaustion exists because smooth vectors have finite orbits under compact \\(H\\). The actual bounded-step bar families are\n\\[\nC_{\\mathrm{cts}}^a(H,R_D\\otimes V)\n=\nR_{D\\times H^a}\\otimes V.\n\\]\nUse the finite-type resolution of Proposition \\(1\\). Each distribution acts on a finite stable span through a finite quotient, so every differential is scalar extension of the differential over \\(\\mathbb F_p\\). Exactness of scalar extension proves the assertion with precisely these finite-span families.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 16", "orig": "Corollary \\(6.8\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( \\Gamma(Y_s,\\mathcal O^\\flat)=\\mathbb C_p^\\flat, \\qquad \\Gamma(Y_s\\times D,\\mathcal O^\\flat)=R_D. \\) For \\(s\\geq1\\), the projection \\(Y_s\\to Y_{s-1}\\) has relative degree-zero direct image \\(\\mathcal O^\\flat\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use \\(Y_s=(N_m^s)_{\\mathrm{ind}}\\) and \\(R_D=C(D,\\mathbb C_p^\\flat)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\Gamma(Y_s,\\mathcal O^\\flat)=\\mathbb C_p^\\flat,\n\\qquad\n\\Gamma(Y_s\\times D,\\mathcal O^\\flat)=R_D.\n\\]\nFor \\(s\\geq1\\), the projection \\(Y_s\\to Y_{s-1}\\) has relative degree-zero direct image \\(\\mathcal O^\\flat\\).</p><p>Consequently, a \\(G\\)-invariant algebraic function in a fixed finite marked model becomes scalar on the completed full tower. If its model and component are defined over \\(k\\), that scalar lies in \\(k\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Over a chart of \\(Y_{s-1}\\) with lifts \\(z_i\\), the last-coordinate inverse image is the untilted line minus\n\\[\nA_z=\\left\\{\\sum_{i=1}^{s-1}a_iz_i+b:a_i\\in\\mathbb Q_p,\\ b\\in F_m\\right\\}.\n\\]\nUse the persistent lattice charts of Lemma \\(14.1\\), choose \\(\\alpha&lt;\\rho&lt;\\beta\\), and exhaust the output line by discs of radius \\(\\rho|p|^{-a}\\). At each bound, graph centers are indexed by the compact lattice \\(p^{-a}L\\); deep tubes are finite disjoint discs indexed by \\(p^{-a}L/p^jL\\), with radii \\(\\alpha|p|^j\\) and separated open companions.</p><p>These meet Proposition \\(12\\). Hence the inverse limit of shrinking support fibers has \\(H^0=H^1=0\\) at each output bound. Increasing the output bound uses the canonical support complexes, not a common tube depth; Proposition \\(12\\) again gives low-degree support vanishing on the whole relative line. The support triangle and Proposition \\(11\\) identify its complement’s functions with base functions.</p><p>Translation descent by \\(F_m\\) takes invariants of these base functions, on which it acts trivially. Induct on \\(s\\), retaining \\(D\\), to obtain the formulas.</p><p>Finite algebraic models inject into their analytic realization and then into the full tower. A \\(G\\)-invariant function descends by Proposition \\(8\\), hence becomes a scalar \\(\\lambda\\). Coefficient-only \\(|k|\\)-Frobenius fixes the original function and sends \\(\\lambda\\) to \\(\\lambda^{|k|}\\); injectivity forces \\(\\lambda^{|k|}=\\lambda\\). Thus \\(\\lambda\\in k\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 17", "orig": "Proposition \\(7.1\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is a natural \\(G\\)-equivariant isomorphism \\( V_{\\operatorname{Coind}_q(E)}\\cong(V_E)^{1/h}. \\) It respects every arrow and exact sequence in finite diagrams, natural auxiliary marking actions, successive …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(1&lt;m&lt;n\\), \\(r=n-m\\), and \\(d=(m-1)r\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(B_\\infty=\\operatorname*{colim}_UB_U\\), and let \\(\\mathcal P\\) be the compatible generator-lift torsor under \\(T=\\varprojlim\\Gamma_m[p^j]^r\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Then\n\\[\n\\mathcal O(\\mathcal P)=B_\\infty^{\\mathrm{perf}},\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal P/[p^\\ell]T=\\operatorname{Spec}B_\\infty^{1/p^{m\\ell}}.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Its distribution algebra is\n\\[\n\\Lambda=\\mathcal O(T)^\\vee\\cong k[[D_1,\\ldots,D_d]].\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Choose \\(a_0&gt;0\\) sufficiently divisible and put\n\\[\nq_0=p^{ma_0},\\quad q=q_0^a,\\quad\n\\ell=(m-1)a_0a,\\quad h=q^{m-1}=p^{m\\ell}.\n\\]\nAll powers fix \\(k\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\Lambda^{(q)}=k[[D_1^q,\\ldots,D_d^q]]\\), and\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Coind}_q(E)=\n\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\Lambda^{(q)}}(\\Lambda,E),\n\\]\nwhere \\(E\\) is transported along \\(D_i\\mapsto D_i^q\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(V_E\\) be the associated bundle at a finite marking stage.</p><p class=\"li\">For an \\(R\\)-module \\(M\\), \\(M^{1/h}\\) means transport along \\(R\\cong R^{1/h}\\), then restriction of scalars along \\(R\\subseteq R^{1/h}\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a natural \\(G\\)-equivariant isomorphism\n\\[\nV_{\\operatorname{Coind}_q(E)}\\cong(V_E)^{1/h}.\n\\]\nIt respects every arrow and exact sequence in finite diagrams, natural auxiliary marking actions, successive coinduction, and successive roots. A fixed initial diagram admits one sufficiently deep marking stage for these identities, retaining the natural actions on all coinductions.</p>", "proof": "<p>Finite-flat Cartier duality identifies \\(\\Lambda\\) with the coordinate algebra of \\((\\Gamma_m^D)^r\\). On the dual Honda group,\n\\[\nF^m=[p]^{m-1}.\n\\]\nIndeed duality gives \\(V^m=[p]\\), and \\(FV=[p]\\) yields the equation after cancellation of faithfully flat \\([p]\\). The dual dimension is \\(m-1\\), since its Frobenius has degree \\(p^{m-1}\\); for \\(m&gt;1\\) it has no étale part. Hence its coordinate algebra is the stated regular power-series ring. The displayed powers identify \\([p^\\ell]\\) on the dual with \\(q\\)-Frobenius, so \\(\\Lambda\\) is free over \\(\\Lambda^{(q)}\\) on \\(D^\\alpha\\), \\(0\\leq\\alpha_i&lt;q\\).</p><p>On the perfect total torsor, absolute powering satisfies\n\\[\n\\rho(f^h)=(\\mathrm{Fr}_h^*\\otimes[p^\\ell]^*)\\rho(f).\n\\]\nThus it identifies the original \\(T\\)-torsor over the Frobenius base with its root torsor having structure group \\([p^\\ell]T\\). The map \\(T\\to[p^\\ell]T\\) is an isomorphism onto that subgroup, not an automorphism onto all of \\(T\\).</p><p>Associated-bundle induction in stages gives\n\\[\nV_{\\operatorname{Coind}_{[p^\\ell]T}^{T}(E)}\n=\n\\pi_*V_E\n\\cong(V_E)^{1/h},\n\\]\nfor the root map \\(\\pi\\). After a faithfully flat torsor trivialization this is evaluation on coinduced equivariant functions, so it respects all arrows and exactness and descends without a chosen torsor point.</p><p>Power identities and coinduction-in-stages prove coherence. All constructions commute with \\(G\\) and marking changes. A finite initial diagram uses finitely many coefficients and relations and therefore descends to one finite stage. The intrinsic power identities descend there with the natural auxiliary actions; no simultaneous trivialization of all high coinductions is asserted.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 18", "orig": "Proposition \\(7.3\\), with the fixed-diagram estimate of Lemma \\(7.4\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "At one finite marking stage there is a \\(G\\)-invariant volume \\(\\eta\\) for which \\( S(f)=\\frac{C^{\\nu_0}(f\\eta)}{\\eta} \\) is continuous, \\(k\\)-linear, \\(G\\)-equivariant, and satisfies \\(S(b^{h_0}f)=bS(f)\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(1\\leq m&lt;n\\), \\(r=n-m\\), and a sufficiently deep finite marked algebra\n\\[\nR=B_U=eA'[1/x],\n\\qquad A=k[[x,y_1,\\ldots,y_{r-1}]].\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(m&gt;1\\), use \\(h_0=q_0^{m-1}\\) from Proposition \\(17\\). For \\(m=1\\), choose \\(h_0=p^{\\nu_0}&gt;1\\) fixing \\(k\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(C\\) be the inverse-linear Cartier operator, with \\(p\\)-basis formula\n\\[\nC^\\nu(f\\,dt)=f_{(p^\\nu-1)\\mathbf1}\\,dt,\n\\quad\nf\\,dt=\\sum_{0\\leq\\alpha_i&lt;p^\\nu}f_\\alpha^{p^\\nu}t^\\alpha dt.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>At one finite marking stage there is a \\(G\\)-invariant volume \\(\\eta\\) for which\n\\[\nS(f)=\\frac{C^{\\nu_0}(f\\eta)}{\\eta}\n\\]\nis continuous, \\(k\\)-linear, \\(G\\)-equivariant, and satisfies \\(S(b^{h_0}f)=bS(f)\\).</p><p>For \\(m&gt;1\\), every nonzero translation augmentation at the \\(q_0^a\\)-level induces a nonzero scalar multiple of \\(S^a\\). For \\(m=1\\), the toral normalization gives\n\\[\nS\\mathrm{Fr}^{\\nu_0}=1.\n\\]</p><p>Extend \\(S\\) to \\(B'=A'[1/x]\\) by \\(S(f)=S(ef)\\). There is fixed \\(c\\geq0\\) such that, for all integers \\(D\\),\n\\[\nS(x^{-D}A')\\subseteq x^{-\\lceil D/h_0\\rceil-c}A'.\n\\]\nThus \\(P=x^{-D_0}A'\\) is \\(S\\)-stable for sufficiently large \\(D_0\\), and all sufficiently high iterates carry any fixed bounded-pole lattice, uniformly on compact families, into \\(P\\).</p><p>The volume, inverse volume, whole trace matrices, and their inverses have one finite pole bound \\(c'\\); their \\(h\\)-root transports have loss at most \\(c'/h\\). Cartier maps are residue-adjoint to inclusions of root Laurent representatives. Fixed finite Yoneda diagrams have one bounded-pole cocycle boundary operator whose coinductions use its transported roots.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma \\(18.1\\) gives equivariance, continuity, and inverse-\\(h_0\\)-linearity. Intermediate volume factors cancel, so\n\\[\nS^a(f)=C^{a\\nu_0}(f\\eta)/\\eta.\n\\]\nFor \\(m&gt;1\\), augmentation functionals are the one-dimensional socle-evaluation line\n\\[\n\\prod_iD_i^{q-1}\\in\\Lambda/(D_1^q,\\ldots,D_d^q).\n\\]\nA pro-\\(p\\) marking group acts trivially on this line. Coinduced successive socle evaluations multiply to\n\\[\n\\prod_iD_i^{q_0^a-1}\\neq0.\n\\]\nProposition \\(17\\) and Lemma \\(18.1\\) identify these with nonzero scalar multiples of \\(S^a\\).</p><p>For \\(m=1\\), the \\(\\mu_p^r\\)-torsor is strongly character-graded. Locally choose unit character generators \\(z_i\\), with \\(b_i=z_i^p\\in R^\\times\\). Their \\(p\\)-basis gives\n\\[\n\\eta=d\\log b_1\\wedge\\cdots\\wedge d\\log b_r.\n\\]\nThese local forms agree on overlaps, and \\(C(\\eta)=\\eta\\). Hence \\(S(f^{h_0})=f\\).</p><p>In the fixed whole basis, \\(T_{\\eta,h_0}\\), composed with \\(e\\), is one finite matrix over \\(A[1/x]\\). Grouping root exponents modulo \\(h_0\\) contributes \\(\\lceil D/h_0\\rceil\\), and the matrix contributes a fixed \\(c\\). Iterating the estimate bounds poles by\n\\[\nh_0^{-a}D+(c+1)\\frac{1-h_0^{-a}}{1-h_0^{-1}}.\n\\]\nChoose \\(D_0&gt;(c+1)/(1-h_0^{-1})\\).</p><p>Write \\(\\eta=a_R(d\\log x\\wedge dy_1\\wedge\\cdots)\\), and extend \\(a_R\\) by \\(1\\) on the complementary component. Finite étale trace makes the whole trace matrices and their inverses localized matrices with one common pole bound. Root transport divides that bound by \\(h\\).</p><p>Coefficient residue in logarithmic \\(x\\) selects exponent \\(0\\), and in ordinary \\(y\\) selects exponent \\(-1\\). The Cartier formula gives\n\\[\n\\langle w,T_{h,hh_0}(f)\\rangle_h\n=\n\\langle\\iota w,f\\rangle_{hh_0}.\n\\]\nFinite étale trace and volume factors respect this identity. No inseparable trace is used. Lemma \\(18.2\\) supplies the final diagram assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 18.1", "orig": "Proposition \\(7.2\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "After one finite marking shrink to a pro-\\(p\\) group, \\(\\widehat\\Omega^1_{R/k}\\) has a \\(G\\)-invariant frame and volume generator \\(\\eta\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use \\(R=B_U\\) from Proposition \\(18\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A translation augmentation is a nonzero invariant functional \\(\\lambda:\\mathcal O(H)\\to k\\), not the Hopf counit.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>After one finite marking shrink to a pro-\\(p\\) group, \\(\\widehat\\Omega^1_{R/k}\\) has a \\(G\\)-invariant frame and volume generator \\(\\eta\\). For a root order \\(h\\) fixing \\(k\\),\n\\[\nT_{\\eta,h}:R^{1/h}\\to R,\n\\qquad\nT_{\\eta,h}(z)=\\frac{C^{\\log_ph}(z^h\\eta)}{\\eta}\n\\]\nis \\(R\\)-linear and generates \\(\\operatorname{Hom}_R(R^{1/h},R)\\) over \\(R^{1/h}\\). A corresponding nonzero translation augmentation induces a scalar multiple of it, with scalar in \\(k\\), after the stated finite constant extension.</p>", "proof": "<p>The order-\\(p\\) root cover is a torsor under \\(\\alpha_p^r\\) for \\(m&gt;1\\), and \\(\\mu_p^r\\) for \\(m=1\\). Shrink the marking group to fix this finite quotient and a Lie basis. Torsor freeness gives a frame \\(\\delta_i\\) of\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Der}_R(R^{1/p},R^{1/p}).\n\\]\nDefine\n\\[\n\\partial_i(b)=\\bigl(\\delta_i(b^{1/p})\\bigr)^p.\n\\]\nThese are \\(k\\)-linear derivations. In a \\(p\\)-basis \\(t_j\\), if \\(\\delta_i=\\sum_ja_{ij}\\partial/\\partial t_j^{1/p}\\), then\n\\[\n\\partial_i=\\sum_ja_{ij}^p\\partial/\\partial t_j,\n\\]\nwhose determinant is a unit. They commute with \\(G\\); their dual frame gives \\(\\eta\\).</p><p>The \\(p\\)-basis Cartier formula follows by tensoring one-variable de Rham computations and iterating. Its inverse characterization \\(db\\mapsto[b^{p-1}db]\\) gives coordinate and étale naturality. It satisfies \\(C^\\nu(b^h\\omega)=bC^\\nu(\\omega)\\).</p><p>For \\(\\eta=dt\\), the pairing \\(T_{\\eta,h}(uv)\\) pairs \\(t^{\\alpha/h}\\) with its complementary basis, hence is perfect. Changing the volume multiplies source and target by units.</p><p>The invariant functional of the finite translation Hopf algebra also has perfect multiplication pairing. Its radical is a translation-stable ideal. A proper such ideal lies in the augmentation ideal; applying \\(\\mathrm{id}\\otimes\\epsilon\\) to its coaction forces the ideal to be zero. Faithfully flat trivialization transfers perfectness to the torsor.</p><p>Thus the two generators differ by\n\\[\n\\tau(z)=T_{\\eta,h}(uz),\n\\qquad u\\in(R^{1/h})^\\times.\n\\]\nEquivariance makes \\(u\\) \\(G\\)-invariant. Proposition \\(16\\), and injectivity into the full perfected tower, make \\(u\\) a scalar in \\(k^\\times\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 18.2", "orig": "Lemma \\(7.4\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Its composite boundary operation has a cocycle representative losing at most one fixed \\(x\\)-pole order.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix a finite Yoneda diagram of translation representations, descended to a finite marking stage, and a cohomological degree.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Its composite boundary operation has a cocycle representative losing at most one fixed \\(x\\)-pole order. Under Proposition \\(17\\), all coinduced diagrams use roots of this same operator and chosen splittings.</p>", "proof": "<p>Every term and image bundle is finite locally free on the affine marked base. Choose underlying module sections for its finitely many surjections, embed projectives in finite free modules, and choose whole lattices. The sections are finite localized matrices with fixed pole loss.</p><p>The compact group action has a uniform lattice bound, unchanged by shifting by \\(x^a\\), because \\(g(x)/x\\) is an integral unit. The finitely many group differentials and sections therefore have a common total loss.</p><p>Proposition \\(17\\) transports the entire diagram, including arrows and chosen sections, to its root diagram. Computing its boundary is exactly the root of the original computation, with no new choices for increasing coinduced ranks.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 19", "orig": "Corollary \\(8.3\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "At every sufficiently deep marking level, \\( H^a(G,W)\\quad\\text{and}\\quad H_i(G,W) \\) are finite-dimensional over \\(k\\), hence finite, for all \\(a,i\\geq0\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain \\(A=k[[x,y_1,\\ldots,y_s]]\\), \\(s=r-1\\), \\(B'=A'[1/x]\\), and \\(B_U=eB'\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n\\[\n\\mathscr E=\\mathbb Z[1/p]\\times(\\mathbb Z[1/p]_{&gt;0})^s,\n\\qquad |J|=\\sum_jJ_j.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(L&gt;\\delta&gt;0\\), set\n\\[\nw_{L,\\delta}(I,J)=\n\\begin{cases}\nLI-\\delta|J|,&amp;I\\geq0,\\\\\n\\delta I-\\delta|J|,&amp;I&lt;0.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathscr L_{L,\\delta}(D)\\) consist of sums \\(\\sum a_{I,J}x^Iy^{-J}\\), \\(a_{I,J}\\in R_D\\), with weighted coefficients\n\\[\n\\|a_{I,J}\\|_{\\sup}e^{-w_{L,\\delta}(I,J)}\\to0.\n\\]\nLet \\(\\mathscr L(D)\\) be the complete-step colimit increasing \\(L\\) and decreasing \\(\\delta\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Multiplication by \\(A[1/x]\\) is followed by projection to strictly negative powers in every \\(y_j\\). Define\n\\[\n\\mathscr W_D=e(\\mathscr L(D)\\otimes_AA'),\n\\qquad\nW_D=e(\\mathscr L(D;k)\\otimes_AA'),\n\\qquad W=W_{\\{*\\}},\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\mathscr L(D;k)\\) has coefficients in \\(C(D,k)\\). The tensor notation denotes the finite whole-basis sum.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>At every sufficiently deep marking level,\n\\[\nH^a(G,W)\\quad\\text{and}\\quad H_i(G,W)\n\\]\nare finite-dimensional over \\(k\\), hence finite, for all \\(a,i\\geq0\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Put\n\\[\nK=R\\Gamma\\!\\left(G,R\\Gamma_c(Z_U,\\mathcal O^\\flat)\\right).\n\\]\nProposition \\(15\\) makes every \\(H^j(K)\\) finite-dimensional over \\(\\mathbb C_p^\\flat\\). Lemmas \\(19.1\\), \\(19.2\\), and finite-type resolution give\n\\[\nK^{\\phi=1}\\simeq R\\Gamma(G,W)[-r].\n\\]</p><p>If \\(V\\) has dimension \\(d\\) over the algebraically closed field \\(\\mathbb C_p^\\flat\\), with invertible \\(|k|\\)-semilinear map \\(\\psi(z)=Az^{[|k|]}\\), then \\(\\psi-1\\) is surjective and its kernel has \\(k\\)-dimension \\(d\\). Indeed its fibers are finite étale of rank \\(|k|^d\\): after multiplying by \\(A^{-1}\\), the equations have leading monomials \\(z_i^{|k|}\\), standard monomial basis of that size, and invertible Jacobian.</p><p>Apply this to \\(H^j(K)\\). The fiber long exact sequence identifies its cohomology with finite \\(\\phi\\)-fixed spaces, proving finite \\(H^a(G,W)\\).</p><p>Proposition \\(6\\) supplies trace elements acting continuously on \\(W\\), and Proposition \\(3\\) gives\n\\[\nH_i(G,W)\\cong H^{n^2-i}(G,W).\n\\]\nThus homology is finite as well.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 19.1", "orig": "Proposition \\(8.1\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Naturally and with uniform compact parameters, \\( R\\Gamma_c(Z_U,\\mathcal O^\\flat;D)\\simeq\\mathscr W_D[-r], \\qquad R\\Gamma_c(Z_U,\\mathcal O^\\flat;D)^{\\phi=1}\\simeq W_D[-r]. \\) Fixed normalized root bases give the same …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use Proposition \\(19\\), and the perfected characteristic-\\(p\\) marked analytic space \\(Z_U\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\phi\\) raise coefficients in \\(\\mathbb C_p^\\flat\\) to \\(|k|\\)-powers, fixing monomial exponents and the whole basis.</p><p class=\"li\">For a complex \\(K\\), put \\(K^{\\phi=1}=\\operatorname{fib}(\\phi-1)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Naturally and with uniform compact parameters,\n\\[\nR\\Gamma_c(Z_U,\\mathcal O^\\flat;D)\\simeq\\mathscr W_D[-r],\n\\qquad\nR\\Gamma_c(Z_U,\\mathcal O^\\flat;D)^{\\phi=1}\\simeq W_D[-r].\n\\]\nFixed normalized root bases give the same module and complete-step category.</p>", "proof": "<p>Use cofinal boxes\n\\[\na\\leq|x|\\leq b,\\qquad |y_j|\\leq\\rho,\\qquad 0&lt;a&lt;b&lt;\\rho&lt;1.\n\\]\nTheir complement has a finite Čech diagram from two \\(x\\)-exteriors and the \\(y\\)-exteriors. Its intersections are products of discs and annuli. Perfectoid closed-domain exhaustion, structural acyclicity, dense Laurent restrictions, and Lemma \\(10.1\\) compute their functions without extra cohomology.</p><p>For each unpunctured \\(y_j\\), nonnegative Laurent powers extend across the disc and form a contractible summand; strictly negative powers survive in degree one. For punctured \\(x\\), subtract the globally extending negative part of the inner function and nonnegative part of the outer function. The surviving pair has convention\n\\[\n(f_+,-f_-),\n\\]\nencoded by one Laurent expression with every \\(x\\)-exponent, including zero once. This sign makes crossings of exponent zero ordinary Laurent operations.</p><p>Iterating the commuting coordinate splittings leaves only degree \\(r=s+1\\). Radius gaps identify convergence with the weights in Proposition \\(19\\); enlarging supports retains coefficients and gives exactly \\(\\mathscr L(D)\\).</p><p>The whole finite étale algebra on each chart is the finite free sum on the fixed basis, already complete and perfectoid. Relative Frobenius identifies its perfection. Taking the chain idempotent \\(e\\) commutes with the support calculation. Pulled-back boxes are cofinal upstairs.</p><p>For every \\(b\\in\\mathbb C_p^\\flat\\), \\(a^{|k|}-a=b\\) has a solution with \\(|a|\\leq|b|\\): use the convergent small solution for \\(|b|&lt;1\\), and roots of norm at most \\(|b|\\) otherwise. Continuous local branches and finite clopen refinement solve this uniformly on \\(D\\). Solving coefficientwise preserves weighted decay; applying \\(e\\) gives surjectivity of \\(\\phi-1\\) on \\(\\mathscr W_D\\), with kernel \\(W_D\\).</p><p>Fixed root bases differ by invertible matrices over finite root localizations with bounded \\(x\\)-poles and no negative \\(y\\)-powers. The multiplication estimate\n\\[\nw_{L,\\delta}(I+A_0,J-B_0)\\geq w_{L,\\delta}(I,J)-Lc\n\\]\nfor coefficients in \\(x^{-c}A\\) makes both changes continuous.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 19.2", "orig": "Lemma \\(8.2\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The actual \\(G\\)-action is jointly continuous and uniformly equicontinuous from a step, with compact group and parameter values, into a common step.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the Laurent complete steps of Proposition \\(19\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The actual \\(G\\)-action is jointly continuous and uniformly equicontinuous from a step, with compact group and parameter values, into a common step. The same holds for marked multiplication, fixed root-basis changes, and completed-resolution matrices. Absolute Frobenius is invertible and additive; powers fixing \\(k\\) commute with \\(G\\) and act as chain automorphisms.</p>", "proof": "<p>Factor an integral formal automorphism preserving \\((x)\\) into\n\\[\nx\\mapsto xu(x,y),\\qquad\ny\\mapsto y+c(x),\\qquad\ny\\mapsto v(x,y),\n\\]\nwhere the last map preserves \\((y)\\) with invertible \\(y\\)-Jacobian, and \\(c(x)\\in xk[[x]]^s\\).</p><p>On a fixed root grid, each surviving output monomial obeys\n\\[\nI'\\geq I,\\qquad |J'|\\leq|J|+(I'-I).\n\\]\nFor translations, every added \\(y\\)-pole requires at least that much \\(x\\)-order. For maps preserving \\((y)\\), finite principal-parts duality and the integral inverse Jacobian give the stronger bound \\(|J'|\\leq|J|\\). These inequalities compose and imply\n\\[\nw_{L,\\delta}(I',J')\\geq w_{L,\\delta}(I,J).\n\\]\nFixed-monomial expansions converge; bounded-weight truncations depend on finitely many jets. Discarding uniformly small tails proves joint continuity on arbitrary convergent series.</p><p>The expansions agree with geometric support pullback on actual collars; for instance \\((y+x)^{-1}\\) expands on \\(|x|&lt;|y|\\). Whole-basis matrices have uniform bounded poles by Proposition \\(4\\); apply the multiplication estimate and then \\(e\\).</p><p>Compact-envelope integration from Proposition \\(1\\) makes distribution actions and comparison homotopies legitimate without closing boundary images. Absolute Frobenius scales exponents and coefficients; root-basis identification and rescaled weights prove continuity of it and its inverse. Powers fixing \\(k\\) are \\(k\\)-linear and commute with the group complexes.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 20", "orig": "Proposition \\(9.2\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "These pairings extend uniquely to an exact sequence of \\(k[G]\\)-modules \\( 0\\longrightarrow W_+\\longrightarrow W\\xrightarrow{\\pi}Q\\longrightarrow0. \\) The map \\(\\pi\\) is continuous on every complete step into discrete …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix the setting of Propositions \\(18\\), \\(19\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Choose an \\(S\\)-stable compact \\(G\\)-lattice \\(P=x^{-D_0}A'\\), with \\(D_0\\) larger than the transfer and trace pole constants.</p><p class=\"li\">Define the discrete countable module\n\\[\nQ=\\operatorname*{colim}\\left(P^\\vee\\xrightarrow{S^\\vee}P^\\vee\\xrightarrow{S^\\vee}\\cdots\\right).\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Definition \\(9.1\\): \\(W_+\\subseteq W\\) consists of expressions having, in some normalized root basis \\(b_{i,h}=b_i^{1/h}\\), a uniform positive \\(x\\)-gap \\(I\\geq\\epsilon&gt;0\\). Root denominators in their series need not be bounded.</p><p class=\"li\">For bounded-root \\(w\\), define\n\\[\n\\lambda_h(w)(p_h)\n=\n\\operatorname{res}_{x_h,y_h}\n\\operatorname{Tr}_{B'^{1/h}/B^{1/h}}(wp_h\\eta_h),\n\\qquad p_h\\in P^{1/h}.\n\\]\nResidue selects \\(x_h\\)-exponent \\(0\\) logarithmically and every \\(y_{j,h}\\)-exponent \\(-1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>These pairings extend uniquely to an exact sequence of \\(k[G]\\)-modules\n\\[\n0\\longrightarrow W_+\\longrightarrow W\\xrightarrow{\\pi}Q\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]\nThe map \\(\\pi\\) is continuous on every complete step into discrete \\(Q\\), including compact parameter families, and is equivariant for the actual support and dual actions. The positive subspace satisfies the complete-step group-action condition.</p>", "proof": "<p>At normalized root level \\(h\\), trace-volume matrices and their inverses have \\(x\\)-pole at most \\(c/h\\). Finite-field decay implies that a series has only finitely many nonzero terms below any fixed \\(x\\)-order. Thus each residue is a continuous finite coefficient test.</p><p>Under powering \\(P^{1/h}\\cong P\\), consecutive compact maps are\n\\[\nT_h(v)=\\bigl(S(v^{hh_0})\\bigr)^{1/h}.\n\\]\nThe Cartier-residue identity of Proposition \\(18\\) gives\n\\[\n\\lambda_{hh_0}(w)=T_h^\\vee\\lambda_h(w).\n\\]\nHence bounded-root expressions define \\(\\pi_{\\mathrm{fin}}\\).</p><p>Testing all elements of \\(P^{1/h}\\), and then multiplying by the inverse trace matrix, gives\n\\[\n\\lambda_h(w)=0\n\\ \\Longrightarrow\\\n\\operatorname{ord}_x(w)&gt;(D_0-c)/h.\n\\]\nConversely a fixed positive gap becomes invisible at sufficiently late root levels, since test and trace poles are \\(O(1/h)\\). Therefore\n\\[\n\\ker\\pi_{\\mathrm{fin}}=W_{\\mathrm{fin}}\\cap W_+.\n\\]</p><p>Every finite coefficient functional is represented by finite scalar principal parts; apply the inverse trace-volume matrix and then \\(e\\). The latter projection does not alter its colimit class after one transpose transition, because transfer lands in the \\(e\\)-component. This proves surjectivity.</p><p>For arbitrary \\(w\\), truncate its finitely many terms below a sufficiently large \\(x\\)-cutoff and project by \\(e\\), obtaining bounded-root \\(v\\) with \\(w-v\\in W_+\\). Define \\(\\pi(w)=\\pi_{\\mathrm{fin}}(v)\\). The bounded-root kernel identity proves independence and exactness.</p><p>A sufficiently small weighted ball has every nonzero monomial of positive \\(x\\)-order, since \\(w_{L,\\delta}(I,J)\\leq LI\\) for \\(I\\geq0\\) and is nonpositive for \\(I&lt;0\\). Thus \\(\\ker\\pi\\) is open in each step. Uniform bounded-\\(x\\) truncation also shows compact parameter images in \\(Q\\) are finite.</p><p>For equivariance, at finite root level identify residue with an intrinsic top local-cohomology class by truncating after multiplying by a large power of \\(x_h\\), then applying the connecting map. The generalized-fraction residue transformation law is invariant under regular-parameter changes. Translations in \\(y\\) give finite expansions modulo that power of \\(x_h\\); the two-ended \\(x\\)-sign convention agrees with the support action. Naturality of trace and invariance of \\(\\eta_h\\) on the \\(e\\)-component yield\n\\[\n\\lambda_h(gw)(p_h)=\\lambda_h(w)(g^{-1}p_h).\n\\]\nDensity and continuity extend this to all \\(W\\).</p><p>Finally, pass to a sufficiently late normalized basis so its uniform group-matrix pole bound is smaller than half the positive gap. Scalar substitutions preserve \\(x\\)-order. This places the full compact group action continuously in one common positive step, also permitting compact-envelope integration.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 21", "orig": "Theorem \\(9.6\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every \\(i\\geq0\\), \\( I_i=\\bigcap_{a\\geq0}S^aM_i \\) is finite.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use \\(P,S,W,W_+,Q\\) from Proposition \\(20\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(M_i=H^i(G,P)\\), a compact \\(k\\)-space.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(i\\geq0\\),\n\\[\nI_i=\\bigcap_{a\\geq0}S^aM_i\n\\]\nis finite. No next-height finiteness hypothesis is required.</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition \\(20\\) and Lemma \\(21.3\\) identify \\(H_i(G,Q)\\) with finite \\(H_i(G,W)\\). Finite-type compact/discrete duality gives\n\\[\nH_i(G,Q)\n=\n\\operatorname*{colim}_{S^\\vee}M_i^\\vee,\n\\qquad\nH_i(G,Q)^\\vee=\\varprojlim_SM_i.\n\\]\nThe inverse limit is finite.</p><p>Its projection to the first term has image \\(I_i\\). Indeed, for \\(x\\in I_i\\), the compact nonempty sets\n\\[\n\\{y\\in S^aM_i:Sy=x\\}\n\\]\nare nested. Their intersection gives a preimage in \\(I_i\\), so \\(S:I_i\\to I_i\\) is surjective, and successive preimages lift \\(x\\) to the inverse limit. Hence \\(I_i\\), a quotient of a finite space, is finite.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 21.1", "orig": "Lemma \\(9.3\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There exist \\(a_*\\geq0\\), one complete weighted step in the original whole basis, and \\(\\epsilon,\\alpha>0\\), such that all \\(\\Phi^{a_*+a}w\\) have \\(x\\)-order at least \\(\\epsilon\\) and …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\Phi(w)=w^{h_0}\\), the \\(k\\)-linear absolute Frobenius automorphism of \\(W\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(w\\) be a finite tuple in \\(W_+\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There exist \\(a_*\\geq0\\), one complete weighted step in the original whole basis, and \\(\\epsilon,\\alpha&gt;0\\), such that all \\(\\Phi^{a_*+a}w\\) have \\(x\\)-order at least \\(\\epsilon\\) and\n\\[\n\\|\\Phi^{a_*+a}w\\|\\leq e^{-\\alpha h_0^a}\n\\qquad(a\\geq0).\n\\]\nNo bound on root denominators is assumed.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose one normalized basis giving a positive gap for all components, and apply an initial Frobenius power clearing that basis denominator. This need not clear series denominators. Integral multiplication preserves the positive gap in the original basis.</p><p>Choose a common step \\((L_1,\\delta)\\). Increasing \\(L_1\\) adds \\((L-L_1)I\\); since \\(I\\geq\\epsilon&gt;0\\), choose \\(L\\) so every nonzero term has weight at least \\(\\alpha&gt;0\\). Frobenius multiplies these weights by \\(h_0^a\\). Integral expansions of the powered whole-basis elements add only nonnegative orders, and negative-\\(y\\) projection improves weights or discards terms. This proves the estimate.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 21.2", "orig": "Lemma \\(9.4\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The subgroup \\(B\\) is open.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(Z\\) be a Polish topological group and \\(B\\subseteq Z\\) an analytic subgroup, meaning a continuous image of a Polish space.</p><p class=\"li\">Assume \\(Z/B\\) is countable.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The subgroup \\(B\\) is open.</p>", "proof": "<p>Analytic subsets of Polish spaces have the Baire property. If \\(B\\) were meagre, its countably many cosets would make \\(Z\\) meagre in itself. Thus \\(B\\) is nonmeagre. Pettis’s theorem makes \\(BB^{-1}=B\\) contain a neighborhood of the identity.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 21.3", "orig": "Lemma \\(9.5\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( H_i(G,W_+)=0\\qquad(i\\geq0). \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the finite-free-resolution homology of Proposition \\(1\\) and the positive Laurent module \\(W_+\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nH_i(G,W_+)=0\\qquad(i\\geq0).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition \\(20\\) gives a termwise exact sequence of homology complexes, since each resolution term is a finite power. The \\(Q\\)-complex is countable in every degree, and \\(H_i(G,W)\\) is finite by Proposition \\(19\\). Thus \\(H_i(G,W_+)\\) is countable.</p><p>Cover \\(W_+\\) by countably many complete positive coefficient spaces \\(E\\), using normalized basis levels, rational weights, rational positive cutoffs, and the closed condition \\(ew=w\\). Each is Polish. In degree \\(i\\), cycles\n\\[\nZ_E=\\{z\\in E^{a_i}:dz=0\\}\n\\]\nare closed Polish subgroups. Their actual boundary subgroup\n\\[\nB_E=Z_E\\cap d(W_+^{a_{i+1}})\n\\]\nhas countable index.</p><p>For each complete primitive step \\(E'\\), the set\n\\[\n\\{(z,u)\\in Z_E\\times(E')^{a_{i+1}}:du=z\\}\n\\]\nis closed, after comparison in a common enlarged step. Its projection is analytic. The countable union over \\(E'\\) is \\(B_E\\), so Lemma \\(21.2\\) makes \\(B_E\\) open.</p><p>Absolute Frobenius and its inverse commute with the completed-resolution differential. Lemma \\(21.1\\) puts iterates of any positive cycle in one \\(Z_E\\), tending to zero. Some iterate is therefore an actual boundary. Apply inverse Frobenius to its primitive. Every cycle bounds.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 22", "orig": "Proposition \\(9.8\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The quotient \\(S^NM_i/S^{N+1}M_i\\) is finite, and \\((S^NM_i)/I_i\\) is a finitely generated \\(k[[z]]\\)-module with \\(z=S\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">In addition to Proposition \\(21\\), assume next-height coefficient finiteness as in Proposition \\(7\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n\\[\nX_i=H^i(G,B_U),\\qquad M_i=H^i(G,P),\n\\]\nand let \\(f_i:M_i\\to X_i\\) be localization followed by \\(e\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(N\\geq1\\), and assume\n\\[\nS^NX_i/S^{N+1}X_i\n\\]\nis countable.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The quotient \\(S^NM_i/S^{N+1}M_i\\) is finite, and \\((S^NM_i)/I_i\\) is a finitely generated \\(k[[z]]\\)-module with \\(z=S\\). The operator \\(S\\) on \\(S^NX_i\\) has countable kernel and cokernel. Moreover,\n\\[\n\\ker(f_i|_{S^NM_i})\n\\]\nis finite and closed, \\(f_i\\) is injective on \\(I_i\\), and\n\\[\n\\bigcap_{a\\geq0}\\operatorname{im}(S^aM_i\\to X_i)\n=\nf_i(I_i)\\cong I_i.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For every \\(a\\geq1\\),\n\\[\nS^aM_i\\to S^aX_i\n\\]\nhas countable kernel and cokernel: apply Proposition \\(7\\) first to whole \\(B'\\), then note that positive transfer powers discard the complementary component.</p><p>Put \\(A_N=S^NM_i\\), \\(Z_N=S^NX_i\\), \\(f=f_i|_{A_N}\\). The induced \\(A_N/SA_N\\to Z_N/SZ_N\\) has countable kernel, using the comparisons at \\(N\\) and \\(N+1\\), and countable image by hypothesis. Thus \\(A_N/SA_N\\) is countable compact Hausdorff, hence finite by Baire. Proposition \\(21\\) and Lemma \\(22.1\\) give finite \\(S\\)-torsion and power-series finite generation.</p><p>Since \\(\\ker f\\) is countable and \\(S\\) has finite fibers on \\(A_N\\), the kernel of \\(S\\) on \\(f(A_N)\\) is countable. The countable quotient \\(Z_N/f(A_N)\\) gives countable kernel on \\(Z_N\\); its cokernel is the assumed consecutive quotient.</p><p>For \\(\\alpha\\in\\ker f\\), represent \\(\\alpha\\) by \\(S^Nc\\), with \\(c\\) a \\(P\\)-valued cocycle. This is \\(e\\)-supported. If \\(i&gt;0\\), its vanishing in \\(X_i\\) gives a bounded-pole primitive \\(b\\). Proposition \\(18\\) puts all sufficiently high \\(S^ab\\) in \\(P\\), so \\(S^a\\alpha=0\\). In degree zero, injectivity of \\(P\\to B'\\) gives the same conclusion immediately. Thus \\(\\ker f\\subseteq T_S(A_N)\\), finite and closed. It meets \\(I_i\\) trivially.</p><p>For a class in every localized compact image, the nested sets\n\\[\n\\{v\\in S^aA_N:f(v)=\\xi\\}\n\\]\nare nonempty finite closed cosets of subgroups of \\(\\ker f\\). Compactness gives a common element in \\(I_i\\), proving the intersection formula without a topology on \\(X_i\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 22.1", "orig": "Lemma \\(9.7\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\(S\\) is an automorphism of \\(I\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(M\\) be a profinite \\(k\\)-space and \\(S:M\\to M\\) continuous linear.</p><p class=\"li\">Assume \\(I=\\bigcap_aS^aM\\) and \\(M/SM\\) are finite.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\(S\\) is an automorphism of \\(I\\). The quotient \\(M/I\\) is finitely generated over \\(k[[z]]\\), \\(z=S\\), and its original topology is \\(z\\)-adic. The locally nilpotent subgroup\n\\[\nT_S(M)=\\bigcup_a\\ker S^a\n\\]\nis finite and closed; in particular \\(\\ker S\\) is finite.</p>", "proof": "<p>Compact preimages show \\(S(I)=I\\), hence invertibility on finite \\(I\\). In \\(V=M/I\\), the nested compact \\(S^aV\\) have zero intersection and shrink uniformly to zero. Thus\n\\[\n\\left(\\sum_jc_jz^j\\right)v=\\lim_t\\sum_{j&lt;t}c_jS^jv\n\\]\ndefines a continuous power-series action.</p><p>Lift a basis of finite \\(V/SV\\). Successive division by \\(S\\) expresses every \\(v\\) as a power-series combination of those lifts, since the residual \\(S^tv^{(t)}\\) tends uniformly to zero. Each \\(V/S^aV\\) is finite; its compact kernel is open, and these kernels form a neighborhood basis.</p><p>Noetherianity stabilizes \\(\\ker z^a\\) in \\(V\\). Their union is finitely generated and killed by a fixed \\(z^t\\), hence finite. The locally nilpotent subgroup of \\(M\\) injects into this union, since \\(S\\) is invertible on \\(I\\), proving finiteness and closedness.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 23", "orig": "Proposition \\(10.3\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every \\(i\\), \\(\\overline T^i(\\Lambda)\\) is constant in \\(\\operatorname{Pro}(\\mathcal A)\\), its canonical projection to \\(T^i(k)\\) is monic, and it is annihilated by \\(\\mathfrak m\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(1&lt;m&lt;n\\), and assume next-height finiteness.</p><p class=\"li\">Choose a cofinal decreasing sequence of deep normal product marking subgroups \\(U_\\nu\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n\\[\nX_\\nu^i=H^i(G,B_{U_\\nu}),\\qquad\nM_\\nu^i=H^i(G,P_\\nu).\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal A_0\\) be \\(k\\)-vector spaces modulo the Serre subcategory of countable-dimensional spaces.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal A\\) be tail families in \\(\\mathcal A_0\\):\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathcal A}(Y,Z)\n=\n\\operatorname*{colim}_{\\nu_0}\n\\prod_{\\nu\\geq\\nu_0}\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathcal A_0}(Y_\\nu,Z_\\nu).\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(\\Lambda=k[[D_1,\\ldots,D_d]]\\), \\(d=(m-1)(n-m)\\), \\(\\mathfrak m=(D_1,\\ldots,D_d)\\), and let \\(\\mathcal C\\) be finite-length \\(\\Lambda\\)-modules.</p><p class=\"li\">Associated bundles define exact cochain-term functors \\(C^j:\\mathcal C\\to\\mathcal A\\), and \\(T^i(E)=H^iC^\\bullet(E)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For finitely generated \\(L\\), set\n\\[\n\\widehat L_{\\mathrm{pro}}=\\{L/\\mathfrak m^aL\\}_a,\\qquad\n\\overline T^i(L)=\\{T^i(L/\\mathfrak m^aL)\\}_a.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(i\\), \\(\\overline T^i(\\Lambda)\\) is constant in \\(\\operatorname{Pro}(\\mathcal A)\\), its canonical projection to \\(T^i(k)\\) is monic, and it is annihilated by \\(\\mathfrak m\\).</p><p>There is \\(N_i\\geq1\\), independent of sufficiently deep \\(\\nu\\), such that\n\\[\nS^{N_i}X_\\nu^i/S^{N_i+1}X_\\nu^i\n\\]\nis countable for every sufficiently large \\(\\nu\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Propositions \\(2\\) and \\(6\\) give a uniform bound \\(c\\) with \\(T^i(E)=0\\) for \\(i&gt;c\\). Artin–Rees makes \\(L\\mapsto\\widehat L_{\\mathrm{pro}}\\) exact, by identifying the induced and intrinsic \\(\\mathfrak m\\)-adic filtrations. Exact cochain-term functors extend exactly to pro-categories, so \\(\\overline T^\\bullet\\) has long exact sequences.</p><p>For allowed \\(q\\), write\n\\[\n\\Lambda_q=\\Lambda/(D_1^q,\\ldots,D_d^q),\\qquad\nN_q=\\Lambda_q^\\vee.\n\\]\nThe perfect coefficient pairing\n\\[\n\\alpha_q(a)(b)=[D_1^{q-1}\\cdots D_d^{q-1}](ab)\n\\]\nidentifies regular quotient transitions with coinduced translation augmentations. Propositions \\(17\\), \\(18\\), with scalar normalizations chosen recursively, give\n\\[\n\\{\\overline T^i(\\Lambda)\\}\n\\cong\n\\{T^i(k)\\xleftarrow S T^i(k)\\xleftarrow S\\cdots\\}.\n\\]\nEach finite comparison is realized on a marking tail; no common trivial auxiliary action is asserted for all \\(q\\).</p><p>Descend on \\(i\\). Assuming constancy above \\(i\\), finite free resolutions and Lemma \\(23.1\\) give constancy of \\(\\overline T^j(L)\\) for \\(j&gt;i\\) and every finitely resolved \\(L\\), in particular \\(\\overline T^{i+1}(\\mathfrak m)\\). Thus the image of\n\\[\n\\overline T^i(\\Lambda)\\to T^i(k)\n\\]\nis the constant kernel of a map to \\(\\overline T^{i+1}(\\mathfrak m)\\).</p><p>This image system is \\(J_a=\\operatorname{im}S^a\\). Lemma \\(23.1\\) gives one finite stabilization index \\(N_i\\) in \\(\\mathcal A\\), yielding the claimed consecutive-image countability on an actual marking tail.</p><p>Proposition \\(22\\) makes \\(S\\) an automorphism of the stabilized image in \\(\\mathcal A\\). Replacing the inverse system by its \\(N_i\\)-th images makes it constant, with monic initial projection. Finally, multiplication by \\(\\mathfrak m\\) followed by augmentation to \\(k\\) is zero; monicity forces its action on \\(\\overline T^i(\\Lambda)\\) to vanish.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 23.1", "orig": "Lemma \\(10.2\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Constant objects in \\(\\operatorname{Pro}(\\mathcal B)\\) are closed under kernels and cokernels of maps between them, and under extensions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal B\\) be an abelian category.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Constant objects in \\(\\operatorname{Pro}(\\mathcal B)\\) are closed under kernels and cokernels of maps between them, and under extensions. If decreasing subobjects \\(J_a\\subseteq X\\) represent a constant subobject \\(I\\subseteq X\\), then \\(J_a=I\\) for all sufficiently large \\(a\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The constant embedding is fully faithful and exact. For extension closure, argue dually in \\(\\operatorname{Ind}(\\mathcal B)\\). In an extension of constants \\(0\\to A\\to E\\to B\\to0\\), write \\(E\\) as a filtered system. The identity of \\(B\\) factors through one image term, giving a stage \\(E_j\\to B\\) that is epic. Push out \\(\\ker(E_j\\to B)\\to E_j\\) along its map to \\(A\\); the pushout maps isomorphically to \\(E\\) by identity on kernel and quotient, and is an object of \\(\\mathcal B\\).</p><p>For the decreasing system, the constant-to-pro map gives compatible inclusions \\(I\\subseteq J_a\\). The quotient system \\(J_a/I\\) is pro-zero. A sufficiently late transition is both monic and zero, so that late quotient vanishes; all later ones vanish as well.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 24", "orig": "Proposition \\(10.5\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is an allowed \\(s=q_0^{a_s}\\) and a generator \\( e_s\\in\\operatorname{Ext}_\\Lambda^d(N_s,k) \\) whose finite Yoneda operation is an epimorphism in \\(\\mathcal A\\):", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use \\(\\Lambda,\\mathcal A,T^\\bullet\\) of Proposition \\(23\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Assume \\(T^\\bullet(k)\\neq0\\), and let \\(b\\) be its largest nonzero degree.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is an allowed \\(s=q_0^{a_s}\\) and a generator\n\\[\ne_s\\in\\operatorname{Ext}_\\Lambda^d(N_s,k)\n\\]\nwhose finite Yoneda operation is an epimorphism in \\(\\mathcal A\\):\n\\[\ne_s:T^{b-d}(N_s)\\twoheadrightarrow T^b(k).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>A composition series gives \\(T^j(E)=0\\) for \\(j&gt;b\\). Thus \\(\\overline T^{b+1}(\\mathfrak m)=0\\), and Proposition \\(23\\) gives\n\\[\nY:=\\overline T^b(\\Lambda)\\cong T^b(k).\n\\]</p><p>Identify \\(N_s\\) with \\(\\Lambda_s\\) as a plain module. Complete the Koszul resolution \\(K_s^\\bullet\\) on \\(D_1^s,\\ldots,D_d^s\\), in degrees \\([-d,0]\\), and apply the exact cochain-term functors. Its spectral sequence is\n\\[\nE_1^{a,j}(s)=\\overline T^j(\\Lambda)^{\\binom d{-a}}\n\\Longrightarrow T^{a+j}(\\Lambda_s).\n\\]\nAll first differentials vanish because \\(\\mathfrak m\\) annihilates the entries.</p><p>For \\(s'&gt;s\\), the inclusion \\(N_s\\to N_{s'}\\) is multiplication by \\(\\prod_jD_j^{s'-s}\\). Its Koszul comparison on the summand indexed by \\(I\\) multiplies by\n\\[\n\\prod_{j\\notin I}D_j^{s'-s}.\n\\]\nHence it is the identity on the top column and zero on all other columns of vertical cohomology. There is no incoming differential at \\((-d,b)\\). Increase \\(s\\) once for each possible later page; naturality and zero target comparisons kill all outgoing differentials on the whole \\(Y\\). Thus the left-edge map is an epimorphism\n\\[\nT^{b-d}(\\Lambda_s)\\twoheadrightarrow Y.\n\\]</p><p>Its composite to \\(T^b(k)\\) is the operation of the top projection\n\\[\nK_s^\\bullet\\to\\Lambda[d]\\to k[d],\n\\]\na generator of \\(\\operatorname{Ext}_\\Lambda^d(\\Lambda_s,k)\\). Lemma \\(24.1\\) gives a finite Yoneda representative. Equality of its derived class with the Koszul map survives exact completion and exact cochain-term functors, so it gives the same operation.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 24.1", "orig": "Lemma \\(10.4\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For every \\(j\\), \\( \\operatorname{Ext}_{\\mathcal C}^j(E,F) \\cong \\operatorname{Ext}_\\Lambda^j(E,F), \\) where the left side is Yoneda Ext with finite-length middle terms.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal C\\) be the finite-length modules over \\(\\Lambda=k[[D_1,\\ldots,D_d]]\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(E,F\\in\\mathcal C\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(j\\),\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Ext}_{\\mathcal C}^j(E,F)\n\\cong\n\\operatorname{Ext}_\\Lambda^j(E,F),\n\\]\nwhere the left side is Yoneda Ext with finite-length middle terms.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(\\mathcal D\\) be \\(\\mathfrak m\\)-power-torsion modules. Every finitely generated submodule has finite length, so \\(\\mathcal D=\\operatorname{Ind}(\\mathcal C)\\).</p><p>An extension with finite endpoints in \\(\\mathcal D\\) has a finite subextension: lift finitely many generators from the right, then generators of successive finite kernels, and include the full left endpoint. The construction can include prescribed finite subcomplexes. Apply it to a finite Yoneda comparison zigzag, processing vertices in an arrow-compatible order, to obtain finite comparisons. Hence finite endpoints have the same Ext in \\(\\mathcal C\\) and \\(\\mathcal D\\).</p><p>Over a Noetherian ring, the \\(\\mathfrak m\\)-power-torsion submodule of an injective module is injective. Iteratively taking such injective embeddings gives an ordinary injective resolution entirely in \\(\\mathcal D\\), computing both \\(\\mathcal D\\)-Ext and ordinary Ext.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 25", "orig": "Lemma \\(10.7\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is one sufficiently deep product marking subgroup \\(U_*\\) such that, for every allowed \\(q\\geq s\\), the composite \\( N_s\\longrightarrow N_q \\xrightarrow{\\operatorname{Coind}_q(e'_0)} N_q[d]\\longrightarrow k[d] \\) …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use \\(s,e_s\\) from Proposition \\(24\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a generator \\(e'_0\\in\\operatorname{Ext}_\\Lambda^d(k,k)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Give coinduced diagrams their natural auxiliary marking actions.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is one sufficiently deep product marking subgroup \\(U_*\\) such that, for every allowed \\(q\\geq s\\), the composite\n\\[\nN_s\\longrightarrow N_q\n\\xrightarrow{\\operatorname{Coind}_q(e'_0)}\nN_q[d]\\longrightarrow k[d]\n\\]\nequals a nonzero scalar multiple of \\(e_s\\) in equivariant Ext for \\(T\\rtimes U_*\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose finite diagrams for \\(e_s,e'_0\\). After an initial shrink \\(U_0\\), these are equivariant with trivial auxiliary actions, agreeing with the natural action at \\(N_s\\). Proposition \\(17\\) gives all coinductions equivariantly without trivializing their growing natural actions. The inclusion \\(N_s\\to N_q\\) is natural; the one-dimensional augmentation line \\(N_q\\to k\\) is fixed by pro-\\(p\\) \\(U_0\\).</p><p>Forget auxiliary actions. The perfect power-subalgebra pairing identifies coinduction with scalar extension. The coinduced top Koszul class becomes the top projection on the \\(D_i^q\\)-Koszul resolution. The comparison from \\(s\\) to \\(q\\) has top coefficient one, so the composite is \\(c_qe_s\\), \\(c_q\\in k^\\times\\).</p><p>All differences lie in the forgetting kernel of the one fixed group\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Ext}_{\\mathcal R_{U_0}}^d(N_s,k).\n\\]\nLemma \\(25.1\\) kills the entire kernel after one shrink, independently of \\(q\\).</p><p>Finite equivariant diagrams descend at a fixed level through a finite faithfully flat quotient of the marked lift torsor; their comparisons therefore give equal operations on actual cohomology at that level and every smaller level.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 25.1", "orig": "Lemma \\(10.6\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Every \\(\\operatorname{Ext}_{\\mathcal R_U}^j(E,F)\\) is finite.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(U\\) be a uniform pro-\\(p\\) marking subgroup.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal R_U\\) be representations of \\(T\\rtimes U\\), rational for \\(T\\) and discrete continuous for \\(U\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(E,F\\) be finite objects.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every \\(\\operatorname{Ext}_{\\mathcal R_U}^j(E,F)\\) is finite. There is one open \\(U'\\subseteq U\\) on which every element of the kernel\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Ext}_{\\mathcal R_U}^j(E,F)\\to\n\\operatorname{Ext}_T^j(E,F)\n\\]\nvanishes.</p>", "proof": "<p>Cofree resolutions for the coalgebra\n\\[\n\\mathcal O(T)\\otimes C_{\\mathrm{lc}}(U,k)\n\\]\ngive the extension-descent spectral sequence\n\\[\nH_{\\mathrm{cts}}^a\\!\\left(U,\\operatorname{Ext}_T^b(E,F)\\right)\n\\Longrightarrow\n\\operatorname{Ext}_{\\mathcal R_U}^{a+b}(E,F).\n\\]\nConjugating the retained \\(T\\)-coordinate makes restriction of a cofree semidirect representation cofree for \\(T\\); its \\(T\\)-invariants are cofree discrete \\(U\\)-representations and acyclic for invariants.</p><p>Lemma \\(24.1\\), the finite Koszul resolution, and composition series make translation Ext finite-dimensional and zero above \\(d\\). Its \\(U\\)-action is discrete: a finite Yoneda representative becomes equivariant with trivial auxiliary action after shrinking to fix its finite translation quotient. Finite-type group resolutions therefore make each spectral-sequence entry, and each fixed-degree abutment, finite.</p><p>Every coalgebra representation is a union of finite-dimensional subrepresentations. Finite endpoints and Yoneda comparisons admit finite diagrams by the same finite-subextension construction. For a class in the forgetting kernel, choose a finite equivariant representative and a finite nonequivariant comparison with zero. Shrink \\(U\\) to trivialize its actions on the original finite diagram and the finite translation quotients of the comparison. Giving comparison terms trivial auxiliary action makes all arrows equivariant. Thus the class vanishes after shrink. The kernel is finite, so intersect finitely many such open subgroups.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 26", "orig": "Theorem \\(10.1\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is \\(\\nu_0\\) such that \\( H_{\\mathrm{cts}}^i(G,B_{U_\\nu}) \\) is countable-dimensional over \\(k\\) for every \\(\\nu\\geq\\nu_0\\) and every integer \\(i\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(1\\leq m&lt;n\\) and assume next-height coefficient finiteness from Proposition \\(7\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Choose a cofinal decreasing sequence \\(U_\\nu\\) of sufficiently deep normal product marking subgroups.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is \\(\\nu_0\\) such that\n\\[\nH_{\\mathrm{cts}}^i(G,B_{U_\\nu})\n\\]\nis countable-dimensional over \\(k\\) for every \\(\\nu\\geq\\nu_0\\) and every integer \\(i\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(m&gt;1\\), use the tail category of Proposition \\(23\\). If all \\(T^i(k)\\) vanish, the uniform finite-isotropy degree bound gives one common vanishing tail.</p><p>Otherwise let \\(b\\) be the largest nonzero degree. Proposition \\(24\\) gives\n\\[\ne_s:T^{b-d}(N_s)\\twoheadrightarrow T^b(k).\n\\]\nIf \\(b-d&lt;0\\), the zero source already contradicts nonzero target. Put \\(j=b-d\\geq0\\). Shrink to the common tail of Propositions \\(23\\), \\(25\\). Proposition \\(22\\) then makes\n\\[\nF_\\nu^b\n=\n\\bigcap_a\\operatorname{im}(S^aM_\\nu^b\\to X_\\nu^b)\n\\]\nfinite.</p><p>At one fixed level \\(U\\), choose a finite diagram for \\(e'_0\\), and underlying module sections once. Lemma \\(18.2\\) gives a cocycle boundary operator\n\\[\n\\mathcal B:Z^j(G,B_U)\\to Z^{j+d}(G,B_U)\n\\]\nwith one fixed pole loss \\(C_U\\). The entire-diagram root comparison and Proposition \\(25\\) yield, for every allowed \\(q=q_0^a\\geq s\\), \\(h=q^{m-1}\\),\n\\[\ne_s[w]=c_{q,U}^{-1}S^a[\\mathcal B(w^h)],\n\\qquad c_{q,U}\\in k^\\times,\n\\]\nwhere \\(w\\) starts at the fixed root level \\(h_s=s^{m-1}\\).</p><p>Proposition \\(7\\), applied to a sufficiently positive whole-lattice shift before projecting by \\(e\\), gives a subspace\n\\[\nZ_U^j\\subseteq H^j(G,B_U^{1/h_s})\n\\]\nwith countable quotient, all of whose classes have strictly positive-order cocycle representatives.</p><p>For such \\(w\\), powers \\(w^h\\) have whole-basis order tending to infinity: first clear the fixed basis root denominator, then use integral multiplication and fixed projection costs. The one fixed loss \\(C_U\\) puts \\(\\mathcal B(w^h)\\) in \\(P_U\\) for all sufficiently large \\(a\\). It remains a \\(P_U\\)-valued cocycle by injectivity into the whole localization. Hence the fixed class \\(e_s[w]\\) belongs to every sufficiently late compact transfer image, and therefore to finite \\(F_\\nu^b\\).</p><p>Thus \\(e_s(Z_U^j)\\) is finite and its entire image is countable. At every level in the tail, its morphism in \\(\\mathcal A\\) is zero, contradicting its epimorphism onto nonzero \\(T^b(k)\\).</p><p>For \\(m=1\\), Proposition \\(18\\) gives \\(S\\mathrm{Fr}^{\\nu_0}=1\\), so \\(S\\) is surjective on every \\(X_\\nu^i\\). Proposition \\(22\\) again gives a finite compact-image intersection. A sufficiently positive whole-lattice cohomology image has countable cokernel by Proposition \\(7\\). Frobenius powers of its cocycle representatives eventually lie in \\(P_\\nu\\), while\n\\[\n[z]=S^a[z^{h_0^a}].\n\\]\nThus that image lies in the finite intersection, and \\(X_\\nu^i\\) is countable. The uniform degree bound supplies one tail for all degrees.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 27", "orig": "Theorem \\(11.1\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every \\(M\\in\\mathcal C_m\\) and \\(i\\geq0\\), \\( H_{\\mathrm{cts}}^i(P_n,M) \\) is finite.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(p,n\\), finite Honda field \\(k\\), \\(G=P_n\\), and\n\\[\nA_m=k[[u_m,\\ldots,u_{n-1}]],\\qquad 1\\leq m\\leq n.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal C_m\\) be precisely the allowed coefficient class of Proposition \\(4\\), with compact topology.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(M\\in\\mathcal C_m\\) and \\(i\\geq0\\),\n\\[\nH_{\\mathrm{cts}}^i(P_n,M)\n\\]\nis finite. In particular this holds for every integer periodicity twist of \\(A_m\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Induct downward on \\(m\\). At \\(m=n\\), every allowed coefficient is finite-dimensional over finite \\(k\\); Proposition \\(1\\)’s degreewise finite free resolution gives finite cohomology. Finite filtrations, sums, and retracts preserve the result.</p><p>At height \\(m&lt;n\\), first treat a basic coefficient \\(M\\). Lemmas \\(27.1\\), \\(27.2\\) give countable \\(H^i(G,M[1/x])\\). Proposition \\(7\\) gives finite cohomology for each strip\n\\[\nx^{-a}M/M.\n\\]\nBounded-denominator cochains identify the quotient \\(M[1/x]/M\\) with the filtered union of these strips, so its cohomology is countable. The exact sequence of Lemma \\(1.1\\) makes \\(H^i(G,M)\\) countable.</p><p>Since \\(M\\) is compact, Proposition \\(1\\) computes this cohomology as a quotient of a closed compact kernel by a compact image. It is compact Hausdorff. A countable compact Hausdorff group is finite by Baire: one singleton has interior, making the group discrete and hence finite.</p><p>Finally use long exact sequences and retracts for every finite construction defining \\(\\mathcal C_m\\). This closes the induction.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 27.1", "orig": "Lemma \\(11.2\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For every sufficiently deep normal product marking level \\(U\\), \\( H^i(G,B_U\\otimes_{A_m}M) \\) is countable in all degrees.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(m&lt;n\\), and assume Proposition \\(27\\)’s coefficient finiteness at height \\(m+1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(M\\) be a basic tensor coefficient at height \\(m\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every sufficiently deep normal product marking level \\(U\\),\n\\[\nH^i(G,B_U\\otimes_{A_m}M)\n\\]\nis countable in all degrees.</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition \\(26\\) gives scalar countability on a marking tail. Proposition \\(5\\) realizes \\(M\\), after trivializing its periodicity line, as a finite translation representation \\(E\\). Any required finite filtration or summand diagram descends after one common shrink, with \\(G\\)-equivariance retained by naturality and faithful descent. Its associated sequences are exact on bounded-denominator cochains by underlying finite-projective sections.</p><p>For \\(m&gt;1\\), \\(E\\) is finite length over \\(\\Lambda\\), and\n\\[\nE\\supseteq\\mathfrak mE\\supseteq\\mathfrak m^2E\\supseteq\\cdots\\supseteq0\n\\]\nhas quotients finite sums of \\(k\\). The associated quotients are finite sums of \\(B_U\\). Their long exact sequences give countability.</p><p>For \\(m=1\\), choose a full finite generator-lift quotient \\(H\\) through which \\(E\\) factors. Its distribution algebra\n\\[\n\\mathcal O(H)^\\vee=\\mathcal O(H^D)\n\\]\nis finite étale over \\(k\\). Every finite module is a summand of finitely many regular blocks; the perfect finite étale trace identifies each block with the regular function representation. Its associated algebra is \\(B_U^{1/h}\\), for a power \\(h\\) fixing \\(k\\), by Proposition \\(5\\). Powering\n\\[\nB_U^{1/h}\\to B_U,\\qquad z\\mapsto z^h\n\\]\nis a \\(k\\)-linear \\(G\\)-equivariant homeomorphism for bounded-pole steps. Thus regular blocks, their finite sums, and summands have countable cohomology.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 27.2", "orig": "Lemma \\(11.3\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Every \\(H^i(G,M_B)\\) is countable, without assuming \\(|H|\\) invertible in \\(k\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Put \\(B=A_m[1/x]\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(M_B\\) be finite locally free over \\(B\\), with its coefficient \\(G\\)-action.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(U\\triangleleft J\\), \\(H=J/U\\), and \\(N=B_U\\otimes_BM_B\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Assume \\(H^i(G,N)\\) is countable in all degrees.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every \\(H^i(G,M_B)\\) is countable, without assuming \\(|H|\\) invertible in \\(k\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Finite étale torsor descent identifies the augmented Amitsur complex with\n\\[\nM_B\\to N\\to C^1(H,N)\\to C^2(H,N)\\to\\cdots.\n\\]\nIt is exact after faithfully flat torsor trivialization, hence exact before base change.</p><p>Choose \\(\\tau\\in B_U\\) with \\(\\operatorname{Tr}(\\tau)=1\\). The \\(B\\)-linear map\n\\[\n\\lambda(z)=\\operatorname{Tr}_{B_U/B}(\\tau z)\n\\]\nsatisfies \\(\\lambda(1)=1\\); applying it to the first factor contracts the augmented Amitsur complex. These are finite localized matrices with bounded pole loss, so the rows remain exact after continuous \\(G\\)-cochains. Equivariance of the contraction is unnecessary.</p><p>The resulting first-quadrant double complex gives\n\\[\nH^b\\!\\left(H,H^a(G,N)\\right)\\Longrightarrow H^{a+b}(G,M_B).\n\\]\nFinite-group cochains are finite products of countable spaces, and each total-degree diagonal is finite. The abutment is countable.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 28", "orig": "Corollary \\(11.4\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every \\(i\\geq0\\) and \\(t\\in\\mathbb Z\\), \\( H_{\\mathrm{cts}}^i(P_n,\\pi_t(E_n/p)), \\qquad H_{\\mathrm{cts}}^i(G_n,\\pi_t(E_n/p)) \\) are finite.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(E_n\\) be Morava \\(E\\)-theory for the height-\\(n\\) Honda group over \\(\\mathbb F_{p^n}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n\\[\nG_n=P_n\\rtimes\\operatorname{Gal}(\\mathbb F_{p^n}/\\mathbb F_p).\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Coefficients carry the compact deformation-parameter topology.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(i\\geq0\\) and \\(t\\in\\mathbb Z\\),\n\\[\nH_{\\mathrm{cts}}^i(P_n,\\pi_t(E_n/p)),\n\\qquad\nH_{\\mathrm{cts}}^i(G_n,\\pi_t(E_n/p))\n\\]\nare finite.</p>", "proof": "<p>The coefficient ring is\n\\[\n\\pi_*E_n=W(\\mathbb F_{p^n})[[u_1,\\ldots,u_{n-1}]][u^{\\pm1}],\n\\qquad |u|=-2.\n\\]\nIt is even and \\(p\\)-torsion-free. Thus odd \\(\\pi_t(E_n/p)\\) vanish; each even group is the special-fiber ring with an integer periodicity-line power.</p><p>Finite extension to the chosen Honda field \\(k\\) identifies it with an \\(m=1\\) coefficient in Proposition \\(27\\). Finite scalar extension commutes with compact cochains and cohomology, being a finite direct sum; its faithfulness gives finiteness over the original field.</p><p>For \\(G_n\\), apply\n\\[\nH^a\\!\\left(\\operatorname{Gal}(\\mathbb F_{p^n}/\\mathbb F_p),\nH^b(P_n,\\pi_t(E_n/p))\\right)\n\\Longrightarrow H^{a+b}(G_n,\\pi_t(E_n/p)).\n\\]\nThe entries are finite and total-degree diagonals are finite. This includes \\(p\\mid n\\); no averaging is used.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 29", "orig": "Proposition \\(12.1\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every \\(j\\in\\mathbb Z\\), \\( \\pi_jL_{K(n)}(S/p) \\) is finite.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(p\\) be any prime and \\(n\\geq1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(S/p\\) be the finite Moore spectrum.</p><p class=\"li\">Use Morava descent\n\\[\nE_2^{s,t}=H_{\\mathrm{cts}}^s(G_n,\\pi_t(E_n/p))\n\\Longrightarrow\\pi_{t-s}L_{K(n)}(S/p).\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">The standard descendability input supplies strong convergence and finite integers \\(R\\geq2,N\\geq0\\) such that \\(E_R^{s,t}=0\\) for \\(s&gt;N\\), uniformly in internal degree. This is a finite-page horizontal bound, not finite cohomological dimension of \\(G_n\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(j\\in\\mathbb Z\\),\n\\[\n\\pi_jL_{K(n)}(S/p)\n\\]\nis finite.</p>", "proof": "<p>The descent and vanishing inputs are those cited from Devinatz–Hopkins, Mathew, and Heard; compatibility with the finite Moore spectrum gives the stated coefficient module.</p><p>Proposition \\(28\\) makes every second-page entry finite, hence every later entry finite. In stem \\(j\\), only \\(0\\leq s\\leq N\\), \\(t=j+s\\), can survive. Strong convergence gives a separated exhaustive filtration. Its tail above \\(N\\) has zero successive quotients and is constant; separatedness makes it zero. The remaining finite filtration has finite successive quotients, so its abutment is finite, including for negative \\(j\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 30", "orig": "Lemma \\(12.2\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "If every \\(\\pi_j(X/p)\\) is finite, then every \\(\\pi_jX\\) is finitely generated over \\(\\mathbb Z_p\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(X\\) be a derived \\(p\\)-complete spectrum:\n\\[\nX\\simeq\\operatorname*{holim}_aX/p^a,\n\\qquad\nX/p^a=\\operatorname{cofib}(p^a:X\\to X).\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">No connectivity is assumed.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>If every \\(\\pi_j(X/p)\\) is finite, then every \\(\\pi_jX\\) is finitely generated over \\(\\mathbb Z_p\\). Conversely, finite generation of all \\(\\pi_jX\\) over \\(\\mathbb Z_p\\) implies finiteness of all \\(\\pi_j(X/p)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The cofiber sequence gives\n\\[\n0\\to\\pi_jX/p\\pi_jX\\to\\pi_j(X/p)\\to(\\pi_{j-1}X)[p]\\to0.\n\\]\nThus each finite middle term is a finite \\(p\\)-group, though not necessarily killed by \\(p\\). The triangles\n\\[\nX/p^a\\to X/p^{a+1}\\to X/p\n\\]\nshow inductively that every \\(\\pi_j(X/p^a)\\) is a finite \\(p\\)-group.</p><p>These inverse systems are Mittag–Leffler, since decreasing images in a finite target stabilize. Their \\(\\varprojlim^1\\) vanish. The Milnor sequence and derived completeness give\n\\[\nM:=\\pi_jX\\cong\\varprojlim_a\\pi_j(X/p^a).\n\\]\nThis makes \\(M\\) a compact Hausdorff pro-\\(p\\) group with its continuous \\(\\mathbb Z_p\\)-module structure. The subgroup \\(pM\\) is compact and closed, and \\(M/pM\\) is finite.</p><p>Choose lifts \\(a_1,\\ldots,a_d\\) of its generators. Successively write\n\\[\nz_\\ell=\\sum_ib_{i,\\ell}a_i+pz_{\\ell+1},\n\\qquad b_{i,\\ell}\\in\\{0,\\ldots,p-1\\}.\n\\]\nThen \\(c_i=\\sum_{\\ell\\geq0}p^\\ell b_{i,\\ell}\\in\\mathbb Z_p\\). The residual \\(p^Lz_L\\) tends to zero: each fixed finite target is killed by a fixed power of \\(p\\), uniformly in \\(z_L\\). Hence \\(z=\\sum_ic_ia_i\\), giving a surjection \\(\\mathbb Z_p^d\\to M\\).</p><p>Conversely, finite generation makes both outer terms of the displayed cofiber short exact sequence finite, and therefore its middle term finite.</p>"}], "edges": [[4, 3, "part"], [6, 5, "part"], [9, 8, "part"], [10, 8, "part"], [12, 11, "part"], [13, 11, "part"], [17, 16, "part"], [18, 16, "part"], [21, 20, "part"], [25, 24, "part"], [27, 26, "part"], [28, 26, "part"], [30, 29, "part"], [34, 33, "part"], [35, 33, "part"], [37, 36, "part"], [38, 36, "part"], [41, 40, "part"], [42, 40, "part"], [43, 40, "part"], [45, 44, "part"], [47, 46, "part"], [49, 48, "part"], [51, 50, "part"], [54, 53, "part"], [55, 53, "part"]]});
